(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqKtg=[{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"There are two vessels filled with an ideal gas where volume of one is double the volume of other. The large vessel contains the gas at 8 kPa at 1000 K while the smaller vessel contains the gas at 7 kPa at 500 K. If the vessels are connected to each other by a thin tube allowing the gas to flow and the temperature of both vessels is maintained at 600 K, at steady state the pressure in the vessels will be (in kPa)",o:["18","6","4.4","24"],c:1,s:`The total number of moles stays the same. Use n = PV/RT. Let the small vessel have volume V and the large one 2V.
Before: n\u2081 = (8 \xD7 2V)/(R \xD7 1000) = 0.016 V/R and n\u2082 = (7 \xD7 V)/(R \xD7 500) = 0.014 V/R (P in kPa).
Total n = 0.030 V/R.
After: both at 600 K and the same pressure P, total volume 3V, so n = (P \xD7 3V)/(R \xD7 600) = 0.005 PV/R.
Equate: 0.005 P = 0.030, so P = 6 kPa.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"For a particular ideal gas which of the following graphs represents the variation of mean squared velocity of the gas molecules with temperature?",fig:{w:340,h:220,alt:"Four graphs of mean squared velocity against temperature: (a) straight line from the origin, (b) rising curve that bends downward, (c) rising curve that bends upward, (d) curve that rises to a peak and then falls",d:[["t",12,18,"(a)","s"],["l",36,90,148,90,"a"],["l",36,90,36,24,"a"],["t",148,102,"T","es"],["t",40,26,"v\xB2","s"],["l",36,90,131,28],["t",182,18,"(b)","s"],["l",206,90,318,90,"a"],["l",206,90,206,24,"a"],["t",318,102,"T","es"],["t",210,26,"v\xB2","s"],["p","M206 90 C216 45 246 28 301 24"],["t",12,124,"(c)","s"],["l",36,196,148,196,"a"],["l",36,196,36,130,"a"],["t",148,208,"T","es"],["t",40,132,"v\xB2","s"],["p","M36 196 Q91 188 131 134"],["t",182,124,"(d)","s"],["l",206,196,318,196,"a"],["l",206,196,206,130,"a"],["t",318,208,"T","es"],["t",210,132,"v\xB2","s"],["p","M206 196 C216 166 226 134 236 134 S256 176 301 192"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`Mean squared velocity: v\xB2 = 3RT/M, where R and M are constants for a given gas.
So v\xB2 is directly proportional to T.
The graph of v\xB2 against T is a straight line through the origin, which is graph (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The ratio of vapour densities of two gases at the same temperature is {4;25}, then the ratio of r.m.s. velocities will be",o:["{25;4}","{5;2}","{4;25}","{2;5}"],c:1,s:`Vapour density is proportional to molar mass M, so M\u2081/M\u2082 = 4/25.
At the same temperature v_rms is proportional to 1/\u221AM.
v\u2081/v\u2082 = \u221A(M\u2082/M\u2081) = \u221A(25/4) = 5/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Graphs of gas processes",q:"P-T diagram of an ideal gas having three different densities \u03C1\u2081, \u03C1\u2082, \u03C1\u2083 (in three different cases) is shown in the figure. Which of the following is correct?",fig:{w:240,h:150,alt:"P against T graph with three straight lines from near the origin; line for density rho 1 is the steepest, rho 2 is in the middle and rho 3 is the least steep",d:[["l",40,125,225,125,"a"],["l",40,125,40,12,"a"],["t",222,141,"T","es"],["t",34,16,"P","e"],["l",40,125,140,22],["l",40,125,172,48],["l",40,125,196,78],["t",142,18,"\u03C1\u2081"],["t",174,46,"\u03C1\u2082"],["t",198,78,"\u03C1\u2083"]]},o:["\u03C1\u2081 > \u03C1\u2082","\u03C1\u2082 < \u03C1\u2083","\u03C1\u2081 < \u03C1\u2082","\u03C1\u2081 = \u03C1\u2082 = \u03C1\u2083"],c:0,s:`For an ideal gas P = \u03C1RT/M, so on a P-T graph the slope is \u03C1R/M.
A larger slope means a larger density.
From the figure, the line of \u03C1\u2081 is steepest, then \u03C1\u2082, then \u03C1\u2083.
So \u03C1\u2081 > \u03C1\u2082 > \u03C1\u2083. Option (a) is correct.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"A sample contains mixture of helium and oxygen gas. The ratio of root mean square speed of helium and oxygen in the sample, is",o:["{1;32}","{1;2\u221A2}","{1;4}","{2\u221A2;1}"],c:3,s:`In the mixture both gases are at the same temperature.
v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M), so v(He)/v(O\u2082) = \u221A(M(O\u2082)/M(He)).
= \u221A(32/4) = \u221A8 = 2\u221A2.
So the ratio is 2\u221A2 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"If the root mean square velocity of hydrogen molecule at a given temperature and pressure is 2 km/s, the root mean square velocity of oxygen at the same condition in km/s is",o:["1.0","1.5","2.0","0.5"],c:3,s:`At the same temperature v_rms is proportional to 1/\u221AM.
v(O\u2082)/v(H\u2082) = \u221A(M(H\u2082)/M(O\u2082)) = \u221A(2/32) = 1/4.
v(O\u2082) = 2 km/s \xD7 1/4 = 0.5 km/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"Two vessels A and B are of the same size and are at same temperature. A contains 1 g of hydrogen and B contains 1 g of oxygen. P_A and P_B are the pressures of the gases in A and B respectively, then {P_A;P_B} is",o:["32","16","4","8"],c:1,s:`For the same V and T, pressure is proportional to the number of moles (P = nRT/V).
Moles of H\u2082 = 1/2 and moles of O\u2082 = 1/32.
P_A/P_B = (1/2)/(1/32) = 16.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"The temperature of a gas having 2.0 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075 molecules per cubic meter at 1.38 atm is (Given, k = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J K\u207B\xB9) is",o:["100 K","300 K","500 K","200 K"],c:2,s:`Use P = nkT, where n is the number of molecules per unit volume.
P = 1.38 atm = 1.38 \xD7 1.01 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa \u2248 1.4 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
T = P/(nk) = (1.4 \xD7 10\u2075)/(2.0 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075 \xD7 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3) = (1.4 \xD7 10\u2075)/276 \u2248 506 K.
This is closest to 500 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"At which temperature the r.m.s velocity of a hydrogen molecule equal to that of an oxygen molecule at 47\xB0C ?",o:["20 K","4 K","\u221273 K","80 K"],c:0,s:`v_rms is proportional to \u221A(T/M), so T/M must be equal for the two gases.
T(H\u2082)/2 = 320/32 (since 47\xB0C = 320 K).
T(H\u2082) = 2 \xD7 10 = 20 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The ratio of speed of sound in hydrogen gas to the speed of sound in oxygen gas at the same temperature is",o:["1 : 4","1 : 2","1 : 1","4 : 1"],c:3,s:`Speed of sound in a gas is v = \u221A(\u03B3RT/M).
Both H\u2082 and O\u2082 are diatomic, so \u03B3 is the same (7/5), and T is the same.
v(H\u2082)/v(O\u2082) = \u221A(M(O\u2082)/M(H\u2082)) = \u221A(32/2) = 4.
The ratio is 4 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The temperature of an ideal gas is increased from 200 K to 800 K. If r.m.s. speed of gas at 200 K is v\u2080. Then, r.m.s. speed of the gas at 800 K will be",o:["4v\u2080","2v\u2080","{v\u2080;4}","v\u2080"],c:1,s:`v_rms is proportional to \u221AT.
v\u2088\u2080\u2080/v\u2082\u2080\u2080 = \u221A(800/200) = \u221A4 = 2.
So the speed at 800 K is 2v\u2080.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"Three vessels of equal volume contain gases at the same temperature and pressure. The first vessel contains neon (monoatomic), the second contains chlorine (diatomic) and third contains uranium hexafluoride (polyatomic). Arrange these on the basis of their root mean square speed (v_rms) and choose the correct answer from the options given below :",o:["v_rms (mono) = v_rms (dia) = v_rms (poly)","v_rms (mono) > v_rms (dia) > v_rms (poly)","v_rms (dia) < v_rms (poly) = v_rms (mono)","v_rms (mono) < v_rms (dia) < v_rms (poly)"],c:1,s:`At the same temperature v_rms is proportional to 1/\u221AM.
Molar masses: neon = 20, chlorine (Cl\u2082) = 71, uranium hexafluoride (UF\u2086) = 352.
The lighter the molecule, the faster it moves.
So v_rms (mono) > v_rms (dia) > v_rms (poly).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The root mean square speed of molecules of nitrogen gas at 27\xB0C is approximately : (Given mass of a nitrogen molecule = 4.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 kg and take Boltzmann constant k_B = 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J K\u207B\xB9)",o:["1260 m/s","523 m/s","27.4 m/s","91 m/s"],c:1,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3k_BT/m) with T = 27\xB0C = 300 K.
3k_BT = 3 \xD7 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 \xD7 300 = 1.26 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 J.
Divide by m: 1.26 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 \xF7 4.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 = 2.74 \xD7 10\u2075.
v_rms = \u221A(2.74 \xD7 10\u2075) \u2248 523 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"If the r.m.s speed of chlorine molecule is 490 m/s at 27\xB0C, the r.m.s. speed of argon molecules at the same temperature will be (Atomic mass of argon = 39.9 u, molecular mass of chlorine = 70.9 u)",o:["651.7 m/s","451.7 m/s","551.7 m/s","751.7 m/s"],c:0,s:`At the same temperature v_rms is proportional to 1/\u221AM.
v(Ar)/v(Cl\u2082) = \u221A(70.9/39.9) = \u221A1.777 \u2248 1.333.
v(Ar) = 490 \xD7 1.333 \u2248 653 m/s.
The closest option is 651.7 m/s (the small gap is due to rounding in the data).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The rms speed of oxygen molecule in a vessel at particular temperature is (1 + {5;x})^(1/2) v, where v is the average speed of the molecule. The value of x will be (Take \u03C0 = {22;7})",o:["28","4","27","8"],c:0,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M) and v_avg = \u221A(8RT/\u03C0M), so v_rms/v_avg = \u221A(3\u03C0/8).
Given (v_rms/v)\xB2 = 1 + 5/x, so 3\u03C0/8 = 1 + 5/x.
With \u03C0 = 22/7: 3\u03C0/8 = 66/56 = 33/28, so 5/x = 5/28.
Hence x = 28.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqm",st:"Maxwell distribution, diffusion and real gases",q:"(P + {a;V\xB2})(V \u2212 b) = RT represents the equation of state of some gases. Where P is the pressure, V is the volume, T is the temperature and a, b, R are the constants. The physical quantity, which has dimensional formula as that of {b\xB2;a}, will be",o:["Bulk modulus","Compressibility","Modulus of rigidity","Energy density"],c:1,s:`In the bracket, a/V\xB2 is added to P, so a/V\xB2 has the dimensions of pressure and a = P V\xB2.
b is subtracted from V, so b has the dimensions of volume.
b\xB2/a = V\xB2/(P V\xB2) = 1/P.
The inverse of pressure is compressibility (1/bulk modulus).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Graphs of gas processes",q:`For three low density gases A, B, C pressure versus temperature graphs are plotted while keeping them at constant volume, as shown in the figure.
The temperature corresponding to the point K is`,fig:{w:340,h:135,alt:"Pressure against temperature in \xB0C for gases A, B and C at constant volume: three straight lines meet on the temperature axis at point K, to the left of 0 \xB0C, and rise with different slopes",d:[["l",30,100,335,100,"a"],["l",110,100,110,14,"a"],["t",104,14,"P (atm)","es"],["t",334,116,"Temperature (\xB0C)","es"],["l",110,96,110,104],["t",110,117,"0\xB0C","ms"],["t",50,117,"K","ms"],["c",50,100,2.5,"F"],["l",50,100,248,28],["l",50,100,248,52],["l",50,100,248,75],["t",254,31,"Gas A","s"],["t",254,55,"Gas B","s"],["t",254,78,"Gas C","s"]]},o:["\u2212373\xB0C","\u221240\xB0C","\u2212100\xB0C","\u2212273\xB0C"],c:3,s:`For a low density (nearly ideal) gas at constant volume, P is proportional to the absolute temperature T.
The lines for all gases meet where the pressure becomes zero.
This point is absolute zero, which is \u2212273\xB0C (about \u2212273.15\xB0C).
So K is at \u2212273\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The root mean square velocity of molecules of gas is",o:["Inversely proportional to square root of temperature (\u221A{1;T})","Proportional to square root of temperature (\u221AT)","Proportional to square of temperature (T\xB2)","Proportional to temperature (T)"],c:1,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3k_BT/m) = \u221A(3RT/M).
For a given gas, 3R/M is constant, so v_rms is proportional to \u221AT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"A bicycle tyre is filled with air having pressure of 270 kPa at 27\xB0C. The approximate pressure of the air in the tyre when the temperature increases to 36\xB0C is",o:["262 kPa","270 kPa","360 kPa","278 kPa"],c:3,s:`The volume of the tyre and the amount of air stay constant, so P/T is constant (T in kelvin).
T\u2081 = 300 K, T\u2082 = 309 K.
P\u2082 = 270 \xD7 309/300 = 278.1 kPa \u2248 278 kPa.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"At 300 K, the rms speed of oxygen molecules is \u221A{\u03B1+5;\u03B1} times to that of its average speed in the gas, Then, the value of \u03B1 will be (used, \u03C0 = {22;7})",o:["28","27","32","24"],c:0,s:`v_rms/v_avg = \u221A(3RT/M) \xF7 \u221A(8RT/\u03C0M) = \u221A(3\u03C0/8).
So (\u03B1 + 5)/\u03B1 = 3\u03C0/8 = (3 \xD7 22)/(7 \xD7 8) = 33/28.
Then 1 + 5/\u03B1 = 1 + 5/28, so \u03B1 = 28.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"The pressure (P) and temperature (T) relationship of an ideal gas obeys the equation PT\xB2 = constant. The volume expansion coefficient of the gas will be",o:["3T\xB2","{3;T\xB2}","{3;T\xB3}","{3;T}"],c:3,s:`From PV = nRT, V = nRT/P.
PT\xB2 = constant gives P = k/T\xB2, so V = nRT \xD7 T\xB2/k, which is proportional to T\xB3.
Volume expansion coefficient \u03B3 = (1/V)(dV/dT) = 3T\xB2/T\xB3 = 3/T.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Sound travels in a mixture of two moles of helium and n moles of hydrogen. If rms speed of gas molecules in the mixture is \u221A2 times the speed of sound, then the value of n will be",o:["1","2","3","4"],c:1,s:`For the mixture, v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M) and speed of sound v = \u221A(\u03B3RT/M), where M is the mean molar mass.
v_rms/v = \u221A(3/\u03B3) = \u221A2, so \u03B3 = 3/2.
Total C_v = 2 \xD7 (3/2)R + n \xD7 (5/2)R = (3 + 2.5n)R, and C_p \u2212 C_v = (2 + n)R.
\u03B3 = 1 + (2 + n)/(3 + 2.5n) = 3/2 gives 2(2 + n) = 3 + 2.5n.
4 + 2n = 3 + 2.5n, so n = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Pressure and kinetic interpretation",q:`Same gas is filled in two vessels of the same volume at the same temperature. If the ratio of the number of molecules is 1 : 4, then
(A) The r.m.s. velocity of gas molecules in two vessels will be the same.
(B) The ratio of pressure in these vessels will be 1 : 4.
(C) The ratio of pressure will be 1 : 1.
(D) The r.m.s. velocity of gas molecules in two vessels will be in the ratio of 1 : 4.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A and C only","B and D only","A and B only","C and D only"],c:2,s:`v_rms depends only on temperature and molar mass. Both are the same, so (A) is true and (D) is false.
P = nkT with the same T, and n (molecules per volume) is in the ratio 1 : 4.
So the pressures are in the ratio 1 : 4: (B) is true and (C) is false.
The correct statements are A and B only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The root mean square speed of smoke particles of mass 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2077 kg in their Brownian motion in air at NTP is approximately. [Given k = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J K\u207B\xB9]",o:["60 mm s\u207B\xB9","12 mm s\u207B\xB9","15 mm s\u207B\xB9","36 mm s\u207B\xB9"],c:2,s:`Smoke particles behave like gas molecules: v_rms = \u221A(3kT/m). At NTP, T = 273 K.
3kT = 3 \xD7 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 \xD7 273 = 1.13 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 J.
Divide by m: 1.13 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 \xF7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2077 = 2.26 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
v_rms = \u221A(2.26 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m/s = 15 mm/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The relation between root mean square speed (v_rms) and most probable speed (v_p) for the molar mass M of oxygen gas molecule at the temperature of 300 K will be",o:["v_rms = \u221A{2;3} v_p","v_rms = \u221A{3;2} v_p","v_rms = v_p","v_rms = \u221A{1;3} v_p"],c:1,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M) and v_p = \u221A(2RT/M).
Dividing: v_rms/v_p = \u221A(3/2).
So v_rms = \u221A(3/2) v_p (true for any gas at any temperature).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"A mixture of hydrogen and oxygen has volume 2000 cm\xB3, temperature 300 K, pressure 100 kPa and mass 0.76 g. The ratio of number of moles of hydrogen to number of moles of oxygen in the mixture will be [Take gas constant R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9]",o:["{1;3}","3","{1;16}","16"],c:1,s:`Total moles n = PV/RT = (100 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 300) = 200/2490 \u2248 0.0803.
Let x = moles of H\u2082 and y = moles of O\u2082: x + y = 0.0803 and 2x + 32y = 0.76.
Substitute x = 0.0803 \u2212 y: 0.1606 + 30y = 0.76, so y \u2248 0.020 and x \u2248 0.060.
x/y = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Graphs of gas processes",q:"For a perfect gas, two pressures P\u2081 and P\u2082 are shown in figure. The graph shows",fig:{w:260,h:140,alt:"Volume against temperature graph with two straight lines through the origin; the line labelled P2 is steeper than the line labelled P1",d:[["l",40,115,235,115,"a"],["l",40,115,40,12,"a"],["t",232,131,"T","es"],["t",34,16,"V","e"],["t",34,127,"O","e"],["l",40,115,200,26],["l",40,115,200,62],["t",204,28,"P\u2082"],["t",204,66,"P\u2081"]]},o:["P\u2081 > P\u2082","P\u2081 < P\u2082","P\u2081 = P\u2082","Insufficient data to draw any conclusion"],c:0,s:`For a fixed amount of gas, V = (nR/P) T, so the slope of a V-T line is nR/P.
A larger slope means a smaller pressure.
The line for P\u2082 is steeper than the line for P\u2081, so P\u2082 < P\u2081.
That is, P\u2081 > P\u2082.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"What will be the effect on the root mean square velocity of oxygen molecules if the temperature is doubled and oxygen molecule dissociates into atomic oxygen?",o:["The velocity of atomic oxygen remains same.","The velocity of atomic oxygen doubles.","The velocity of atomic oxygen becomes half.","The velocity of atomic oxygen becomes four times."],c:1,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M).
Temperature doubles (T \u2192 2T) and the molar mass halves (O\u2082 = 32 \u2192 O = 16, M \u2192 M/2).
New v_rms = \u221A(3R \xD7 2T/(M/2)) = \u221A(4 \xD7 3RT/M) = 2 \xD7 old v_rms.
The velocity doubles.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"A vessel contains 16 g of hydrogen and 128 g of oxygen at standard temperature and pressure. The volume of the vessel in cm\xB3 is",o:["72 \xD7 10\u2075","32 \xD7 10\u2075","27 \xD7 10\u2074","54 \xD7 10\u2074"],c:2,s:`Moles of H\u2082 = 16/2 = 8 and moles of O\u2082 = 128/32 = 4, so the total is 12 mol.
At STP one mole of an ideal gas occupies 22.4 L.
V = 12 \xD7 22.4 = 268.8 L = 2.688 \xD7 10\u2075 cm\xB3 \u2248 27 \xD7 10\u2074 cm\xB3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"A cylindrical container of volume 4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 contains one mole of hydrogen and two moles of carbon dioxide. Assume the temperature of the mixture is 400 K. The pressure of the mixture of gases is [Take gas constant as 8.3 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9]",o:["24.9 \xD7 10\xB3 Pa","249 \xD7 10\xB9 Pa","24.9 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa","24.9 Pa"],c:2,s:`Total moles n = 1 + 2 = 3 (Dalton's law: the mixture obeys PV = nRT with the total n).
P = nRT/V = (3 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 400)/(4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3).
= 9960/(4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 2.49 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa = 24.9 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"An ideal gas is expanding such that PT\xB3 = constant. The coefficient of volume expansion of the gas is",o:["{2;T}","{3;T}","{1;T}","{4;T}"],c:3,s:`From PV = nRT, V is proportional to T/P.
PT\xB3 = constant means P is proportional to T\u207B\xB3, so V is proportional to T \xD7 T\xB3 = T\u2074.
Coefficient of volume expansion = (1/V)(dV/dT) = 4T\xB3/T\u2074 = 4/T.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"A mixture of hydrogen and oxygen has volume 500 cm\xB3, temperature 300 K, pressure 400 kPa and mass 0.76 g. The ratio of masses of oxygen to hydrogen will be",o:["16 : 3","3 : 8","8 : 3","3 : 16"],c:0,s:`Total moles n = PV/RT = (400 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)/(8.3 \xD7 300) = 200/2490 \u2248 0.0803 (R = 8.3).
Let x = moles of H\u2082 and y = moles of O\u2082: x + y = 0.0803 and 2x + 32y = 0.76.
Solving gives y \u2248 0.020 and x \u2248 0.060, so x : y = 3 : 1.
Mass of H\u2082 = 3 \xD7 2 = 6 units, mass of O\u2082 = 1 \xD7 32 = 32 units.
Mass of O\u2082 : mass of H\u2082 = 32 : 6 = 16 : 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"Consider a mixture of gas molecule of types A, B and C having masses m_A < m_B < m_C. The ratio of their root mean square speeds at normal temperature and pressure is",o:["v_A = v_B \u2260 v_C","{1;v_A} < {1;v_B} < {1;v_C}","v_A = v_B = v_C = 0","{1;v_A} > {1;v_B} > {1;v_C}"],c:1,s:`All the gases are at the same temperature, and v_rms = \u221A(3kT/m).
The lighter the molecule, the larger its speed: v_A > v_B > v_C.
Taking reciprocals reverses the order: 1/v_A < 1/v_B < 1/v_C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Graphs of gas processes",q:"Which of the following graphs represent the behavior of an ideal gas? Symbols have their usual meaning.",fig:{w:340,h:220,alt:"Four graphs of PV against T: (a) horizontal line, (b) falling hyperbola-like curve, (c) falling straight line, (d) rising straight line through the origin",d:[["t",12,18,"(a)","s"],["l",36,90,148,90,"a"],["l",36,90,36,24,"a"],["t",148,102,"T","es"],["t",40,26,"PV","s"],["l",38,42,131,42],["t",182,18,"(b)","s"],["l",206,90,318,90,"a"],["l",206,90,206,24,"a"],["t",318,102,"T","es"],["t",210,26,"PV","s"],["p","M210 28 Q220 82 301 86"],["t",12,124,"(c)","s"],["l",36,196,148,196,"a"],["l",36,196,36,130,"a"],["t",148,208,"T","es"],["t",40,132,"PV","s"],["l",38,134,131,188],["t",182,124,"(d)","s"],["l",206,196,318,196,"a"],["l",206,196,206,130,"a"],["t",318,208,"T","es"],["t",210,132,"PV","s"],["l",206,196,301,136]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`For an ideal gas PV = nRT.
For a fixed amount of gas, PV is directly proportional to T.
So the graph of PV against T is a straight line through the origin with a positive slope, which is graph (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The number of molecules in one litre of an ideal gas at 300 K and 2 atmospheric pressure with mean kinetic energy 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 J per molecule is",o:["6 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9","3 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9","0.75 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9","1.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9"],c:3,s:`Total translational kinetic energy of the gas is E = (3/2)PV.
P = 2 atm = 2 \xD7 1.013 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa and V = 1 L = 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3.
E = 1.5 \xD7 2.026 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u2248 304 J.
Number of molecules N = E/(KE per molecule) = 304/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079) \u2248 1.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"One mole of an ideal gas is taken through an adiabatic process where the temperature rises from 27\xB0C to 37\xB0C. If the ideal gas is composed of polyatomic molecule that has 4 vibrational modes, which of the following is true ? [R = 8.314 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9]",o:["Work done on the gas is close to 332 J","Work done by the gas is close to 332 J","Work done on the gas is close to 582 J","Work done by the gas is close to 582 J"],c:2,s:`Degrees of freedom: 3 translational + 3 rotational + 2 \xD7 4 vibrational (each mode counts 2) = 14.
So C_v = (14/2)R = 7R.
In an adiabatic process Q = 0, so the work done on the gas equals the rise in internal energy.
W = \u0394U = nC_v\u0394T = 1 \xD7 7 \xD7 8.314 \xD7 10 \u2248 582 J.
The temperature rises, so work is done on the gas.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"The volume V of an enclosure contains a mixture of three gases, 16 g of oxygen, 28 g of nitrogen and 44 g of carbon dioxide at absolute temperature T. Consider R as universal gas constant. The pressure of the mixture of gases is",o:["{4RT;V}","{3RT;V}","{88RT;V}","{5;2} {RT;V}"],c:3,s:`Moles: O\u2082 = 16/32 = 0.5, N\u2082 = 28/28 = 1, CO\u2082 = 44/44 = 1.
Total n = 2.5 = 5/2.
P = nRT/V = (5/2) RT/V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"Consider a sample of oxygen behaving like an ideal gas. At 300 K, the ratio of root mean square (rms) velocity to the average velocity of gas molecule would be (Molecular weight of oxygen is 32 g/mol; R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9)",o:["\u221A{3;3}","\u221A{8\u03C0;3}","\u221A{3\u03C0;8}","\u221A{8;3}"],c:2,s:`v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M) and v_avg = \u221A(8RT/\u03C0M).
Ratio = \u221A(3RT/M) \xF7 \u221A(8RT/\u03C0M) = \u221A(3\u03C0/8).
The ratio does not depend on the gas or its temperature.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"For an adiabatic expansion of an ideal gas, the fractional change in its pressure is equal to (where \u03B3 is the ratio of specific heats)",o:["\u2212\u03B3 {V;dV}","\u2212{1;\u03B3} {dV;V}","\u2212\u03B3 {dV;V}","{dV;V}"],c:2,s:`For an adiabatic process PV^\u03B3 = constant.
Take the logarithm: ln P + \u03B3 ln V = constant.
Differentiate: dP/P + \u03B3 dV/V = 0.
Fractional change in pressure dP/P = \u2212\u03B3 dV/V.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Pressure and kinetic interpretation",q:"On the basis of kinetic theory of gases, the gas exerts pressure because its molecules",o:["suffer change in momentum when impinge on the walls of container.","continuously stick to the walls of container.","continuously lose their energy till it reaches wall.","are attracted by the walls of container."],c:0,s:`Molecules strike the walls and rebound elastically.
Each collision changes the momentum of the molecule, so the wall receives an impulse.
The average force per unit area from the huge number of collisions is the pressure.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"The internal energy (U), pressure (P) and volume (V) of an ideal gas are related as U = 3PV + 4. The gas is",o:["monoatomic only","diatomic only","polyatomic only","either monoatomic or diatomic"],c:2,s:`For an ideal gas U = (f/2) nRT = (f/2) PV (plus any constant).
Comparing with U = 3PV + 4 gives f/2 = 3, so f = 6.
Only polyatomic (non-linear) gases have 6 degrees of freedom (3 translational + 3 rotational).
So the gas is polyatomic only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:`The temperature, at which the root mean square velocity of hydrogen molecules equals their escape velocity from the earth, is closest to
[Boltzmann constant, k_B = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J/K
Avogadro Number, N_A = 6.02 \xD7 10\xB2\u2076 / kg
Radius of Earth : 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 m
Gravitational acceleration on Earth = 10 m s\u207B\xB2]`,o:["10\u2074 K","800 K","650 K","3 \xD7 10\u2075 K"],c:0,s:`Escape velocity v_e = \u221A(2gR), and v_rms = \u221A(3k_BT/m). Set them equal: 3k_BT/m = 2gR.
Mass of a hydrogen molecule m = 2/(6.02 \xD7 10\xB2\u2076) kg = 3.32 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg (N_A is per kg-mole).
T = 2gRm/(3k_B) = (2 \xD7 10 \xD7 6.4 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 3.32 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077)/(3 \xD7 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3).
= (4.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)/(4.14 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3) \u2248 1.0 \xD7 10\u2074 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"An HCl molecule has rotational, translational and vibrational motions. If the rms velocity of HCl molecules in its gaseous phase is v\u0304, m is its mass k_B is Boltzmann constant, then its temperature will be",o:["{mv\u0304\xB2;3k_B}","{mv\u0304\xB2;7k_B}","{mv\u0304\xB2;5k_B}","{mv\u0304\xB2;6k_B}"],c:0,s:`The rms speed depends only on the translational motion: v_rms = \u221A(3k_BT/m).
Rotation and vibration do not change the relation between v_rms and T.
Squaring gives v\u0304\xB2 = 3k_BT/m, so T = mv\u0304\xB2/(3k_B).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"For a given gas at 1 atm pressure, rms speed of the molecules is 200 m/s at 127\xB0C. At 2 atm pressure and at 227\xB0C, the rms speed of the molecules will be",o:["80\u221A5 m/s","100\u221A5 m/s","80 m/s","100 m/s"],c:1,s:`v_rms depends only on temperature (not on pressure): v_rms is proportional to \u221AT.
T\u2081 = 127 + 273 = 400 K and T\u2082 = 227 + 273 = 500 K.
v\u2082 = 200 \xD7 \u221A(500/400) = 200 \xD7 \u221A5/2 = 100\u221A5 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Maxwell distribution, diffusion and real gases",q:"The number density of molecules of a gas depends on their distance r from the origin as, n(r) = n\u2080e^(\u2212\u03B1r\u2074). Then the total number of molecules is proportional to",o:["n\u2080\u03B1^(\u22123/4)","n\u2080\u03B1^(1/4)","n\u2080\u03B1\u207B\xB3","\u221A(n\u2080) \u03B1^(1/2)"],c:0,s:`Count the molecules in thin spherical shells of volume 4\u03C0r\xB2 dr.
N = \u222B n\u2080 e^(\u2212\u03B1r\u2074) 4\u03C0r\xB2 dr, from r = 0 to infinity.
Put u = \u03B1^(1/4) r, so r = u \u03B1^(\u22121/4) and r\xB2 dr = u\xB2 du \u03B1^(\u22123/4).
Then N = 4\u03C0 n\u2080 \u03B1^(\u22123/4) \u222B u\xB2 e^(\u2212u\u2074) du, and the integral is just a number.
So N is proportional to n\u2080 \u03B1^(\u22123/4).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"A mixture of 2 moles of helium gas (atomic mass = 4 u), and 1 mole of argon gas (atomic mass = 40 u) is kept at 300 K in a container. The ratio of their rms speeds {v\u1D63\u2098\u209B(helium);v\u1D63\u2098\u209B(argon)} is close to",o:["2.24","0.32","0.45","3.16"],c:3,s:`Both gases are at the same temperature, so v\u1D63\u2098\u209B = \u221A(3RT/M) depends only on the molar mass.
The amounts (2 mol and 1 mol) do not matter.
Ratio = \u221A(M_Ar/M_He) = \u221A(40/4) = \u221A10 \u2248 3.16.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"Two kg of a monoatomic gas is at a pressure of 4 \xD7 10\u2074 N/m\xB2. The density of the gas is 8 kg/m\xB3. What is the order of energy of the gas due to its thermal motion?",o:["10\u2074 J","10\xB3 J","10\u2076 J","10\u2075 J"],c:0,s:`Volume V = mass/density = 2/8 = 0.25 m\xB3.
For a monoatomic gas the thermal (internal) energy is U = (3/2)PV.
U = 1.5 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 0.25 = 1.5 \xD7 10\u2074 J.
The order of magnitude is 10\u2074 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"An ideal gas occupies a volume of 2 m\xB3 at a pressure of 3 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa. The energy of the gas is",o:["9 \xD7 10\u2076 J","3 \xD7 10\xB2 J","6 \xD7 10\u2074 J","10\u2078 J"],c:0,s:`The energy of the gas is its internal (thermal) energy. Taking the gas as monoatomic, U = (3/2)nRT = (3/2)PV.
U = 1.5 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 2 = 9 \xD7 10\u2076 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"A vertical closed cylinder is separated into two parts by a frictionless piston of mass m and negligible thickness. The piston is free to move along the length of the cylinder. The length of the cylinder above the piston is l\u2081 and that below the piston is l\u2082, such that l\u2081 > l\u2082. Each part of the cylinder contains n moles of an ideal gas at equal temperature T. If the piston is stationary, its mass, m, will be given by (R is universal gas constant and g is the acceleration due to gravity)",o:["{RT;ng}[{l\u2081 \u2212 3l\u2082;l\u2081 l\u2082}]","{nRT;g}[{1;l\u2082} + {1;l\u2081}]","{RT;g}[{2l\u2081 + l\u2082;l\u2081 l\u2082}]","{nRT;g}[{l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082;l\u2081 l\u2082}]"],c:3,s:`Let A be the cross-section. Upper pressure P\u2081 = nRT/(A l\u2081), lower pressure P\u2082 = nRT/(A l\u2082).
The piston is in equilibrium: (P\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081)A = mg.
mg = nRT(1/l\u2082 \u2212 1/l\u2081) = nRT (l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082)/(l\u2081 l\u2082).
So m = {nRT;g}[{l\u2081 \u2212 l\u2082;l\u2081 l\u2082}].`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:`The value closest to the thermal velocity of a Helium atom at room temperature (300 K) in m s\u207B\xB9 is
[k_B = 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J/K; m_He = 7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg]`,o:["1.3 \xD7 10\xB3","1.3 \xD7 10\u2075","1.3 \xD7 10\xB2","1.3 \xD7 10\u2074"],c:0,s:`Thermal (rms) speed v = \u221A(3k_BT/m).
v = \u221A(3 \xD7 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 \xD7 300 / 7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077) = \u221A(1.8 \xD7 10\u2076).
v \u2248 1.34 \xD7 10\xB3 m/s, closest to 1.3 \xD7 10\xB3 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"The temperature of an open room of volume 30 m\xB3 increases from 17\xB0C to 27\xB0C due to the sunshine. The atmospheric pressure in the room remains 1 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa. If N\u1D62 and N_f are the number of molecules in the room before and after heating, then N_f \u2212 N\u1D62 will be",o:["\u22121.61 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3","1.38 \xD7 10\xB2\xB3","2.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075","\u22122.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075"],c:3,s:`Number of molecules N = PV/(k_BT), with P and V fixed.
N_f \u2212 N\u1D62 = (PV/k_B)(1/300 \u2212 1/290) = (3 \xD7 10\u2076/1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3)(\u22121.15 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074).
= 2.17 \xD7 10\xB2\u2079 \xD7 (\u22121.15 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) \u2248 \u22122.5 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075.
Molecules leave the open room as it warms, so the change is negative.`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqg",st:"Graphs of gas processes",q:"Which of the following shows the correct relationship between the pressure \u2018P\u2019 and density \u03C1 of an ideal gas at constant temperature?",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Four graphs of pressure P against density \u03C1: (a) a falling hyperbola-like curve, (b) a falling straight line, (c) a rising curve bending over, (d) a rising straight line through the origin.",d:[["l",35,95,155,95,"a"],["l",35,95,35,23,"a"],["t",95,15,"(a)","m"],["t",155,107,"\u03C1","es"],["t",40,31,"P","s"],["t",27,107,"O","s"],["l",205,95,325,95,"a"],["l",205,95,205,23,"a"],["t",265,15,"(b)","m"],["t",325,107,"\u03C1","es"],["t",210,31,"P","s"],["t",197,107,"O","s"],["l",35,200,155,200,"a"],["l",35,200,35,128,"a"],["t",95,120,"(c)","m"],["t",155,212,"\u03C1","es"],["t",40,136,"P","s"],["t",27,212,"O","s"],["l",205,200,325,200,"a"],["l",205,200,205,128,"a"],["t",265,120,"(d)","m"],["t",325,212,"\u03C1","es"],["t",210,136,"P","s"],["t",197,212,"O","s"],["p","M43 31 Q49 85 147 89"],["l",213,33,317,87],["p","M35 200 Q55 140 147 138"],["l",205,200,317,140]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`PV = nRT gives P = (m/V)(RT/M) = \u03C1RT/M.
At constant T, P is directly proportional to \u03C1.
The graph is a straight line through the origin, rising: graph (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqk",st:"Pressure and kinetic interpretation",q:"In an ideal gas at temperature T, the average force that a molecule applies on the walls of a closed container depends on T as T\u1D60. A good estimate for q is",o:["2","1","{1;2}","{1;4}"],c:1,s:`Force = change of momentum per collision / time between collisions.
Change of momentum = 2mv \u221D v, and time between collisions = 2L/v \u221D 1/v.
So the average force F \u221D v \xD7 v = v\xB2 \u221D T.
Hence q = 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"An open glass tube is immersed in mercury in such a way that a length of 8 cm extends above the mercury level. The open end of the tube is then closed and sealed and the tube is raised vertically up by additional 46 cm. What will be length of the air column above mercury in the tube now? (Atmospheric pressure = 76 cm of Hg)",o:["6 cm","16 cm","22 cm","38 cm"],c:1,s:`Trapped air: P\u2081 = 76 cm Hg, length 8 cm.
After raising, the tube top is 8 + 46 = 54 cm above the pool. Let air length be x; the mercury column in the tube is (54 \u2212 x) cm.
Air pressure P\u2082 = 76 \u2212 (54 \u2212 x) = 22 + x.
Boyle: (22 + x)x = 76 \xD7 8 = 608, so x\xB2 + 22x \u2212 608 = 0, giving x = 16 cm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"An ideal monoatomic gas is confined in a cylinder by a spring loaded piston of cross section 8.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2. Initially the gas is at 300 K and occupies a volume of 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 and the spring is in its relaxed state as shown in figure. The gas is heated by a small heater until the piston moves out slowly by 0.1 m. The force constant of the spring is 8000 N/m and the atmospheric pressure is 1.0 \xD7 10\u2075 N/m\xB2. The cylinder and the piston are thermally insulated. The piston and the spring are massless and there is no friction between the piston and the cylinder. (Neglect the heat loss through the lead wires of the heater. The heat capacity of the heater coil is also negligible). The final temperature of the gas will be:",fig:{w:340,h:140,alt:"Horizontal cylinder with gas on the right, a heater coil inside, and a piston on the left joined by a relaxed spring to a fixed wall.",d:[["l",30,45,290,45],["l",30,105,290,105],["l",290,45,290,105,"B"],["l",30,40,30,110,"B"],["p","M30 75 L40 75 L46 61 L58 89 L70 61 L82 89 L94 61 L106 89 L112 75 L122 75"],["l",122,45,122,105,"B"],["t",76,32,"spring (relaxed)","ms"],["t",205,72,"gas","m"],["p","M235 90 L240 80 L250 100 L260 80 L270 100 L278 90"],["t",256,122,"heater","ms"],["t",122,125,"piston","ms"],["t",30,125,"wall","ms"]]},o:["300 K","800 K","500 K","1000 K"],c:1,s:`Initially the spring is relaxed, so P\u2081 = P\u2080 = 1.0 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa, V\u2081 = 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3.
Finally V\u2082 = 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 + 8.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.1 = 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3.
P\u2082 = P\u2080 + kx/A = 10\u2075 + (8000 \xD7 0.1)/(8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
T\u2082 = T\u2081 (P\u2082V\u2082)/(P\u2081V\u2081) = 300 \xD7 (2 \xD7 3.2)/(1 \xD7 2.4) = 800 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:`A thin tube sealed at both ends is 100 cm long. It lies horizontally, the middle 20 cm containing mercury and two equal ends containing air at standard atmospheric pressure. If the tube is now turned to a vertical position, by what amount will the mercury be displaced?
(Given : cross section of the tube can be assumed to be uniform)`,fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Horizontal sealed tube 100 cm long with a 20 cm mercury column in the middle and air of length l\u2080 on each side.",d:[["r",20,40,300,40],["r",140,40,60,40,"F"],["t",170,64,"Hg","m"],["t",80,64,"air","m"],["t",260,64,"air","m"],["l",20,100,140,100,"b"],["t",80,115,"l\u2080","m"],["l",200,100,320,100,"b"],["t",260,115,"l\u2080","m"],["l",140,128,200,128,"b"],["t",170,143,"20 cm","m"],["l",20,155,320,155,"b"],["t",170,171,"100 cm","m"]]},o:["2.95 cm","5.18 cm","8.65 cm","0.0 cm"],c:1,s:`Each air column is l\u2080 = 40 cm at 76 cm Hg. When vertical, the mercury moves down by x: upper air = 40 + x, lower air = 40 \u2212 x.
Boyle for each: P_up(40 + x) = P_low(40 \u2212 x) = 76 \xD7 40 = 3040.
The mercury (20 cm) is supported: P_low \u2212 P_up = 20.
3040[1/(40 \u2212 x) \u2212 1/(40 + x)] = 20 gives x\xB2 + 304x \u2212 1600 = 0.
x = (\u2212304 + 314.35)/2 \u2248 5.18 cm.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"Two non-reactive monoatomic ideal gases have their atomic masses in the ratio 2 : 3. The ratio of their partial pressures, when enclosed in a vessel kept at a constant temperature, is 4 : 3. The ratio of their densities is",o:["1 : 4","1 : 2","6 : 9","8 : 9"],c:3,s:`From PV = (m/M)RT, density \u03C1 = PM/(RT).
Same T, so \u03C1\u2081/\u03C1\u2082 = (P\u2081M\u2081)/(P\u2082M\u2082) = (4 \xD7 2)/(3 \xD7 3) = 8/9.
Ratio of densities = 8 : 9.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"A thermally insulated vessel contains an ideal gas of molecular mass M and ratio of specific heats \u03B3. It is moving with speed v and is suddenly brought to rest. Assuming no heat is lost to the surroundings, its temperature increases by",o:["{(\u03B3 \u2212 1);2(\u03B3 + 1)R}Mv\xB2 K","{(\u03B3 \u2212 1);2\u03B3R}Mv\xB2 K","{\u03B3Mv\xB2;2R} K","{(\u03B3 \u2212 1);2R}Mv\xB2 K"],c:3,s:`Per mole, the kinetic energy of the moving gas, \xBDMv\xB2, becomes internal energy.
\xBDMv\xB2 = C_V \u0394T, with C_V = R/(\u03B3 \u2212 1).
\u0394T = {(\u03B3 \u2212 1)Mv\xB2;2R} K.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"Three perfect gases at absolute temperatures T\u2081, T\u2082 and T\u2083 are mixed. The masses of molecules are m\u2081, m\u2082 and m\u2083 and the number of molecules are n\u2081, n\u2082 and n\u2083 respectively. Assuming no loss of energy, the final temperature of the mixture is",o:["{(T\u2081 + T\u2082 + T\u2083);3}","{n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082 + n\u2083T\u2083;n\u2081 + n\u2082 + n\u2083}","{n\u2081T\u2081\xB2 + n\u2082T\u2082\xB2 + n\u2083T\u2083\xB2;n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082 + n\u2083T\u2083}","{n\u2081\xB2T\u2081\xB2 + n\u2082\xB2T\u2082\xB2 + n\u2083\xB2T\u2083\xB2;n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082 + n\u2083T\u2083}"],c:1,s:`The total energy is conserved. For each molecule the thermal energy is (f/2)kT; taking the same energy per molecule per kelvin for all three gases, energy \u221D nT.
n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082 + n\u2083T\u2083 = (n\u2081 + n\u2082 + n\u2083)T.
T = (n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082 + n\u2083T\u2083)/(n\u2081 + n\u2082 + n\u2083).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pqm",st:"Maxwell distribution, diffusion and real gases",q:"A real gas behaves like an ideal gas if its",o:["pressure and temperature are both high","pressure and temperature are both low","pressure is high and temperature is low","pressure is low and temperature is high"],c:3,s:`Ideal behaviour needs molecules to be far apart (so their volume and attraction can be ignored) and moving fast.
This happens at low pressure and high temperature.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"At what temperature is the r.m.s. velocity of a hydrogen molecule equal to that of an oxygen molecule at 47\xB0C?",o:["80 K","\u221273 K","3 K","20 K"],c:3,s:`v\u1D63\u2098\u209B \u221D \u221A(T/M), so T_H/M_H = T_O/M_O.
T_O = 47 + 273 = 320 K.
T_H = 320 \xD7 (2/32) = 20 K.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"Cooking gas containers are kept in a lorry moving with uniform speed. The temperature of the gas molecules inside will",o:["increase","decrease","remain same","decrease for some, while increase for others"],c:2,s:`Temperature depends on the random motion of molecules relative to the container, not on the motion of the container as a whole.
The uniform speed of the lorry adds nothing to the random kinetic energy, so the temperature remains the same.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"pqg",st:"Graphs of gas processes",q:"An ideal gas is initially at temperature T and volume V. Its volume is increased by \u0394V due to an increase in temperature \u0394T, pressure remaining constant. The quantity \u03B4 = {\u0394V;V\u0394T} varies with temperature as",fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Four graphs of \u03B4 against T with marks at T and T+\u0394T: (a) a horizontal line, (b) a rising straight line, (c) a falling curve, (d) a rising curve that steepens.",d:[["l",35,85,160,85,"a"],["l",35,85,35,23,"a"],["t",97,15,"(a)","m"],["t",160,111,"T","es"],["t",40,31,"\u03B4","s"],["l",65,85,65,90],["l",105,85,105,90],["t",65,102,"T","ms"],["t",105,102,"T+\u0394T","ms"],["l",205,85,330,85,"a"],["l",205,85,205,23,"a"],["t",267,15,"(b)","m"],["t",330,111,"T","es"],["t",210,31,"\u03B4","s"],["l",235,85,235,90],["l",275,85,275,90],["t",235,102,"T","ms"],["t",275,102,"T+\u0394T","ms"],["l",35,195,160,195,"a"],["l",35,195,35,133,"a"],["t",97,125,"(c)","m"],["t",160,221,"T","es"],["t",40,141,"\u03B4","s"],["l",65,195,65,200],["l",105,195,105,200],["t",65,212,"T","ms"],["t",105,212,"T+\u0394T","ms"],["l",205,195,330,195,"a"],["l",205,195,205,133,"a"],["t",267,125,"(d)","m"],["t",330,221,"T","es"],["t",210,141,"\u03B4","s"],["l",235,195,235,200],["l",275,195,275,200],["t",235,212,"T","ms"],["t",275,212,"T+\u0394T","ms"],["l",55,45,135,45],["l",225,75,305,37],["p","M55 143 Q63 185 140 187"],["p","M225 187 Q295 183 310 145"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`At constant pressure V = (nR/P)T, so V \u221D T.
Then \u0394V/\u0394T = V/T, so \u03B4 = {\u0394V;V\u0394T} = 1/T.
\u03B4 falls as T rises, following a hyperbola: graph (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"A vessel contains 1 mole of O\u2082 gas (molar mass 32) at a temperature T. The pressure of the gas is P. An identical vessel containing one mole of He gas (relative molar mass 4) at a temperature 2T has a pressure of",o:["P/8","P","2P","8P"],c:2,s:`Same volume and same number of moles, so P \u221D T (molar mass does not matter).
The He is at 2T, so its pressure is 2P.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The temperature of an ideal gas is increased from 120 K to 480 K. If at 120 K the root-mean-square velocity of the gas molecules is v, at 480 K it becomes",o:["4v","2v","v/2","v/4"],c:1,s:`v\u1D63\u2098\u209B \u221D \u221AT.
T becomes 480/120 = 4 times, so v\u1D63\u2098\u209B becomes \u221A4 = 2 times.
The new speed is 2v.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:`Three closed vessels A, B and C are at the same temperature T and contain gases which obey the Maxwellian distribution of velocities. Vessel A contains only O\u2082, B only N\u2082 and C a mixture of equal quantities of O\u2082 and N\u2082. If the average speed of the O\u2082 molecules in vessel A is v\u2081 that of the N\u2082 molecules in vessel B is v\u2082, the average speed of the O\u2082 molecules in vessel C is
(M is the mass of an oxygen molecule.)`,o:["{v\u2081 + v\u2082;2}","v\u2081","(v\u2081\xB7v\u2082)^(1/2)","\u221A{3kT;M}"],c:1,s:`Average speed of a molecule depends only on temperature and its own mass: v = \u221A(8k_BT/\u03C0m).
In a mixture every kind of molecule keeps its own distribution at the common temperature.
So O\u2082 in vessel C has the same average speed as O\u2082 in vessel A, which is v\u2081.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"If one mole of a monoatomic gas (\u03B3 = 5/3) is mixed with one mole of a diatomic gas (\u03B3 = 7/5), the value of \u03B3 for the mixture is",o:["1.40","1.50","1.53","3.07"],c:1,s:`Monoatomic: C_V = 3R/2, C_P = 5R/2. Diatomic: C_V = 5R/2, C_P = 7R/2.
For the mixture (1 mol each): C_V = (3R/2 + 5R/2)/2 = 2R, C_P = (5R/2 + 7R/2)/2 = 3R.
\u03B3 = C_P/C_V = 3R/2R = 1.5.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"At room temperature, the rms speed of the molecules of a certain diatomic gas is found to be 1930 m/s. The gas is",o:["H\u2082","F\u2082","O\u2082","Cl\u2082"],c:0,s:`M = 3RT/v\xB2. Take T \u2248 300 K: M = 3 \xD7 8.31 \xD7 300/(1930)\xB2 \u2248 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg/mol = 2 g/mol.
This is the molar mass of hydrogen, so the gas is H\u2082.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"The left and right compartments of a thermally isolated container of length L are separated by a thermally conducting, movable piston of area A. The left and right compartments are filled with 3/2 and 1 moles of an ideal gas, respectively. In the left compartment the piston is attached by a spring with spring constant k and natural length 2L/5. In thermodynamic equilibrium, the piston is at a distance L/2 from the left and right edges of the container as shown in the figure. Under the above conditions, if the pressure in the right compartment is P = (kL/A)\u03B1, then the value of \u03B1 is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Container of length L split by a movable conducting piston at its middle; the left part holds 3/2 mol with a spring joining piston and left wall, the right part holds 1 mol.",d:[["r",30,50,280,70],["l",170,50,170,120,"B"],["p","M30 85 L50 85 L58 70 L72 100 L86 70 L100 100 L114 70 L128 100 L142 70 L156 85 L170 85"],["l",30,28,310,28,"b"],["t",170,20,"L","m"],["l",30,138,170,138,"b"],["l",170,138,310,138,"b"],["t",100,153,"L/2","m"],["t",240,153,"L/2","m"],["t",100,112,"3/2 mol","ms"],["t",240,88,"1 mol","m"]]},n:.2,s:`The conducting piston makes both gases have the same temperature T. Both volumes are AL/2.
So P_left/P_right = (3/2)/1, i.e. P_left = 1.5P with P the right pressure.
The spring is stretched by L/2 \u2212 2L/5 = L/10 and pulls the piston to the left with force kL/10.
Balance: P_left A = P A + kL/10, so 0.5PA = kL/10, giving P = kL/(5A).
\u03B1 = 1/5 = 0.2.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"A spherical soap bubble inside an air chamber at pressure P\u2080 = 10\u2075 Pa has a certain radius so that the excess pressure inside the bubble is \u0394P = 144 Pa. Now, the chamber pressure is reduced to 8P\u2080/27 so that the bubble radius and its excess pressure change. In this process, all the temperatures remain unchanged. Assume air to be an ideal gas and the excess pressure \u0394P in both the cases to be much smaller than the chamber pressure. The new excess pressure \u0394P in Pa is ______.",n:96,s:`Since \u0394P is tiny, the pressure of the air in the bubble is almost equal to the chamber pressure.
Isothermal: P r\xB3 = constant, so P\u2080 r\u2080\xB3 = (8P\u2080/27) r\xB3, giving (r/r\u2080)\xB3 = 27/8 and r = 1.5 r\u2080.
Excess pressure of a soap bubble \u0394P = 4S/r \u221D 1/r.
New \u0394P = 144/1.5 = 96 Pa.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`0.056 kg of nitrogen is enclosed in a vessel at a temperature of 127\xB0C. The amount of heat required to double the speed of its molecules is ______ kcal.
(Take R = 2 cal mole\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)`,n:12,s:`Moles n = 56 g/28 = 2. Speed \u221D \u221AT, so doubling the speed makes T four times: 400 K \u2192 1600 K, \u0394T = 1200 K.
The vessel is rigid, so Q = nC_V\u0394T with C_V = (5/2)R = 5 cal/mol K for nitrogen.
Q = 2 \xD7 5 \xD7 1200 = 12000 cal = 12 kcal.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:"When a gas filled in a closed vessel is heated by raising the temperature by 1\xB0C, its pressure increases by 0.4%. The initial temperature of the gas is ______ K.",n:250,s:`Constant volume: P/T = constant, so \u0394P/P = \u0394T/T.
0.4/100 = 1/T, giving T = 250 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The root mean square speed of molecules of a given mass of a gas at 27\xB0C and 1 atmosphere pressure is 200 ms\u207B\xB9. The root mean square speed of molecules of the gas at 127\xB0C and 2 atmosphere pressure is {x;\u221A3} ms\u207B\xB9. The value of x will be ______.",n:400,s:`v\u1D63\u2098\u209B = \u221A(3RT/M) depends only on T (not on pressure).
v\u2082 = 200 \u221A(400/300) = 200 \xD7 2/\u221A3 = 400/\u221A3 m/s.
So x = 400.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"The volume V of a given mass of monoatomic gas changes with temperature T according to the relation V = KT^(2/3). The workdone when temperature changes by 90 K will be xR. The value of x is ______. [R = universal gas constant]",n:60,s:`Take 1 mole of gas. PV = RT, so work dW = P dV = RT (dV/V).
From V = KT^(2/3): dV/V = (2/3) dT/T.
dW = RT \xD7 (2/3)(dT/T) = (2/3)R dT.
W = (2/3)R \xD7 90 = 60R, so x = 60.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"Nitrogen gas is at 300\xB0C temperature. The temperature in (K) at which the rms speed of a H\u2082 molecule would be equal to the rms speed of a nitrogen molecule, is ______. (Molar mass of N\u2082 gas is 28 g)",n:41,s:`T_N = 300 + 273 = 573 K.
Equal rms speeds: T_H/M_H = T_N/M_N.
T_H = 573 \xD7 2/28 \u2248 40.9 K \u2248 41 K.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"A thermally isolated cylindrical closed vessel of height 8 m is kept vertically. It is divided into two equal parts by a diathermic (perfect thermal conductor) frictionless partition of mass 8.3 kg. Thus the partition is held initially at a distance of 4 m from the top as shown in the schematic figure. Each of the two parts of the vessel contains 0.1 mole of an ideal gas at temperature 300 K. The partition is now released and moves without any gas leaking from one part of the vessel to the other. When equilibrium is reached, the distance of the partition from the top (in m) will be ______ (take the acceleration due to gravity = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and the universal gas constant = 8.3 J mol\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB9).",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Vertical cylinder 8 m high with a partition 4 m from the top, each half holding 0.1 mol of gas at 300 K.",d:[["r",60,20,100,150],["l",60,95,160,95,"B"],["l",185,20,185,95,"b"],["t",192,62,"4 m"],["l",230,20,230,170,"b"],["t",238,98,"8 m"],["t",110,52,"0.1 mol","ms"],["t",110,70,"300 K","ms"],["t",110,135,"0.1 mol","ms"],["t",110,153,"300 K","ms"]]},n:6,s:`Let the partition end at x m from the top. The diathermic partition keeps both gases at 300 K.
Top pressure P\u2081 = nRT/(Ax), bottom pressure P\u2082 = nRT/(A(8 \u2212 x)).
Equilibrium: (P\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081)A = mg, so nRT[1/(8 \u2212 x) \u2212 1/x] = mg.
nRT = 0.1 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 300 = 249 and mg = 83, so (2x \u2212 8)/(x(8 \u2212 x)) = 1/3.
This gives x\xB2 \u2212 2x \u2212 24 = 0, so x = 6 m.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:`A cylindrical furnace has height (H) and diameter (D) both 1 m. It is maintained at temperature 360 K. The air gets heated inside the furnace at constant pressure P\u2090 and its temperature becomes T = 360 K. The hot air with density \u03C1 rises up a vertical chimney of diameter d = 0.1 m and height h = 9 m above the furnace and exits the furnace (see the figure). As a result, atmospheric air of density \u03C1\u2090 = 1.2 kg m\u207B\xB3, pressure P\u2090 and temperature T\u2090 = 300 K enters the furnace. Assume air as an ideal gas, neglect the variations in \u03C1 and T inside the chimney and the furnace. Also ignore the viscous effects.
[Given: The acceleration due to gravity g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and \u03C0 = 3.14]
Considering the air flow to be streamline, the steady mass flow rate of air exiting the chimney is ______ gm s\u207B\xB9.`,fig:{w:340,h:275,alt:"Furnace of height H and diameter D with a narrow vertical chimney of diameter d and height h on top, and an inlet pipe bringing in outside air at the bottom.",d:[["r",110,170,80,70],["l",145,40,145,170],["l",155,40,155,170],["l",150,36,150,12,"a"],["l",30,212,110,212],["l",30,232,110,232],["l",30,222,95,222,"a"],["t",30,204,"\u03C1\u2090, P\u2090, T\u2090","s"],["t",150,206,"\u03C1, P, T","ms"],["t",150,224,"furnace","ms"],["l",215,170,215,240,"b"],["t",222,208,"H"],["l",110,252,190,252,"b"],["t",150,267,"D","m"],["l",185,40,185,170,"b"],["t",192,108,"h"],["t",165,36,"d","s"]]},n:47.1,s:`At constant pressure \u03C1 \u221D 1/T, so \u03C1 = 1.2 \xD7 300/360 = 1.0 kg m\u207B\xB3.
The pressure difference driving the flow is (\u03C1\u2090 \u2212 \u03C1)gh = 0.2 \xD7 10 \xD7 9 = 18 Pa.
Bernoulli: \xBD\u03C1v\xB2 = 18, so v = 6 m/s.
Mass flow = \u03C1 A v = 1.0 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 (0.05)\xB2 \xD7 6 = 0.0471 kg/s = 47.1 g/s.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"pqg",st:"Ideal gas equation and gas laws",q:`A cylindrical furnace has height (H) and diameter (D) both 1 m. It is maintained at temperature 360 K. The air gets heated inside the furnace at constant pressure P\u2090 and its temperature becomes T = 360 K. The hot air with density \u03C1 rises up a vertical chimney of diameter d = 0.1 m and height h = 9 m above the furnace and exits the furnace (see the figure). As a result, atmospheric air of density \u03C1\u2090 = 1.2 kg m\u207B\xB3, pressure P\u2090 and temperature T\u2090 = 300 K enters the furnace. Assume air as an ideal gas, neglect the variations in \u03C1 and T inside the chimney and the furnace. Also ignore the viscous effects.
[Given: The acceleration due to gravity g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and \u03C0 = 3.14]
When the chimney is closed using a cap at the top, a pressure difference \u0394P develops between the top and the bottom surfaces of the cap. If the changes in the temperature and density of the hot air, due to the stoppage of air flow, are negligible then the value of \u0394P is ______ N m\u207B\xB2.`,fig:{w:340,h:275,alt:"Furnace of height H and diameter D with a narrow vertical chimney of diameter d and height h on top, and an inlet pipe bringing in outside air at the bottom.",d:[["r",110,170,80,70],["l",145,40,145,170],["l",155,40,155,170],["l",150,36,150,12,"a"],["l",30,212,110,212],["l",30,232,110,232],["l",30,222,95,222,"a"],["t",30,204,"\u03C1\u2090, P\u2090, T\u2090","s"],["t",150,206,"\u03C1, P, T","ms"],["t",150,224,"furnace","ms"],["l",215,170,215,240,"b"],["t",222,208,"H"],["l",110,252,190,252,"b"],["t",150,267,"D","m"],["l",185,40,185,170,"b"],["t",192,108,"h"],["t",165,36,"d","s"]]},n:18,s:`The air at the furnace level is at P\u2090 on both sides (furnace is open to outside air).
Inside the chimney the pressure at the cap is P\u2090 \u2212 \u03C1gh; outside at the same height it is P\u2090 \u2212 \u03C1\u2090gh.
\u0394P = (\u03C1\u2090 \u2212 \u03C1)gh = (1.2 \u2212 1.0) \xD7 10 \xD7 9 = 18 N m\u207B\xB2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement-I : The average momentum of a molecule in a sample of an ideal gas depends on temperature.
Statement-II : The rms speed of oxygen molecules in a gas is v. If the temperature is doubled and the oxygen molecules dissociate into oxygen atoms, the rms speed will become 2v.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statements-I and II are true and statement-II is a correct explanation for statement-I","Statements-I and II are true and statement-II is not a correct explanation for statement-I","Statement-I is true, statement-II is false","Statement-I is false, statement-II is true"],c:3,s:`Statement-I: molecules move in all directions equally, so the average (vector) momentum is zero at every temperature. It does not depend on temperature, so I is false.
Statement-II: v \u221D \u221A(T/M). T doubles (\xD72) and M halves (\xD7\xBD) on dissociation, so the speed factor is \u221A(2 \xD7 2) = 2. So II is true.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"pqg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A closed container of volume 0.02 m\xB3 contains a mixture of neon and argon gases, at a temperature of 27\xB0C and pressure of 1 \xD7 10\u2075 N m\u207B\xB2. The total mass of the mixture is 28 g. If the molar masses of neon and argon are 20 and 40 g mol\u207B\xB9 respectively, find the masses of the individual gases in the container assuming them to be ideal (Universal gas constant R = 8.314 J/mol-K).",a:"Neon: 4 g, Argon: 24 g",s:`Total moles n = PV/RT = (10\u2075 \xD7 0.02)/(8.314 \xD7 300) = 0.80 mol.
Let the mass of neon be x g: x/20 + (28 \u2212 x)/40 = 0.80.
Multiply by 40: 2x + 28 \u2212 x = 32, so x = 4 g (using n \u2248 0.80).
Mass of argon = 28 \u2212 4 = 24 g.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"pqg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two glass bulbs of equal volume are connected by a narrow tube and are filled with a gas at 0\xB0C and a pressure of 76 cm of mercury. One of the bulbs is then placed in melting ice and the other is placed in a water bath maintained at 62\xB0C. What is the new value of the pressure inside the bulbs? The volume of the connecting tube is negligible.",a:"83.75 cm of Hg",s:`The total number of moles stays the same: P(2V)/273 = P\u2032V/273 + P\u2032V/335.
(335 K = 62 + 273.)
P\u2032 = 2P/(1 + 273/335) = 152/1.815.
P\u2032 \u2248 83.75 cm of Hg.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"pqg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A jar contains a gas and a few drops of water at T K. The pressure in the jar is 830 mm of Hg. The temperature of the jar is reduced by 1%. The saturated vapour pressures of water at the two temperatures are 20 and 25 mm of Hg. Calculate the new pressure in the jar.",a:"817 mm of Hg",s:`Water vapour is saturated, so at T the dry gas pressure = 830 \u2212 25 = 805 mm (25 mm belongs to the higher temperature T).
Dry gas cools at constant volume: P \u221D T, so new dry pressure = 805 \xD7 0.99 = 796.95 mm.
Add the saturated vapour pressure at the lower temperature (20 mm).
New pressure = 796.95 + 20 \u2248 817 mm of Hg.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The root mean square (rms) speed of oxygen molecules (O\u2082) at a certain temperature T (degree absolute) is v. If the temperature is doubled and oxygen gas dissociates into atomic oxygen, the rms speed remains unchanged.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`v\u1D63\u2098\u209B \u221D \u221A(T/M).
T doubles and M halves (O\u2082 \u2192 O), so the speed becomes \u221A(2 \xD7 2) v = 2v.
It does not stay unchanged, so the statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Two different gases at the same temperature have equal root mean square velocities.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`v\u1D63\u2098\u209B = \u221A(3RT/M). At the same T it depends on the molar mass.
Different gases have different molar masses, so their rms speeds differ. The statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"pqg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The volume V versus temperature T graphs for a certain amount of a perfect gas at two pressures P\u2081 and P\u2082 are as shown in figure. It follows from the graphs that P\u2081 is greater than P\u2082.",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Two straight lines of volume against temperature from the origin; the line for P\u2081 is steeper than the line for P\u2082.",d:[["l",40,160,300,160,"a"],["l",40,160,40,15,"a"],["t",300,176,"T","es"],["t",32,22,"V","e"],["l",40,160,250,50],["t",256,50,"P\u2081"],["l",40,160,250,100],["t",256,104,"P\u2082"],["t",32,172,"O","e"]]},o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`V = (nR/P)T, so the slope of a V\u2013T line is nR/P.
A larger pressure gives a smaller slope.
The P\u2081 line is steeper, so P\u2081 < P\u2082. The statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The root-mean square speeds of the molecules of different ideal gases, maintained at the same temperature are the same.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`v\u1D63\u2098\u209B = \u221A(3RT/M) depends on the molar mass M as well as T.
At the same T, lighter gases have larger rms speeds. The statement is false.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pqg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A ring shaped tube contains two ideal gases with equal masses and molar masses M\u2081 = 32 and M\u2082 = 28. The gases are separated by one fixed partition and another movable stopper S which can move freely without friction inside the ring. The angle \u03B1 as shown in the figure is ______ degree.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"Ring-shaped tube with a fixed partition at the top and a movable stopper S; gas M\u2081 is on the left and gas M\u2082 on the right, and the angle \u03B1 is measured from the partition round the M\u2082 side to S.",d:[["c",170,100,85],["c",170,100,62],["l",170,15,170,38,"B"],["l",152.3,183.1,157.1,160.6,"B"],["c",170,100,2,"F"],["l",170,100,170,38,"d"],["l",170,100,157.1,160.6,"d"],["p","M170 75 A25 25 0 1 1 164.8 124.5","a"],["t",200,105,"\u03B1","s"],["t",130,20,"M\u2081","m"],["t",210,20,"M\u2082","m"],["t",130.3,191.1,"S","s"]]},a:"\u03B1 = 192\xB0",s:`The stopper is free, so the two gases have the same pressure; they are also at the same temperature.
Equal masses: n \u221D 1/M, and with the same P and T, volume \u221D n. So V\u2081/V\u2082 = M\u2082/M\u2081 = 28/32 = 7/8.
Volume is proportional to the angle occupied in the ring: \u03B8\u2081 + \u03B8\u2082 = 360\xB0 with \u03B8\u2081:\u03B8\u2082 = 7:8, so \u03B8\u2081 = 168\xB0 and \u03B8\u2082 = 192\xB0.
The angle \u03B1 is the one occupied by gas M\u2082, so \u03B1 = 192\xB0.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"pqg",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"During an experiment, an ideal gas is found to obey an additional law VP\xB2 = constant. The gas is initially at a temperature T, and volume V. When it expands to a volume 2V, the temperature becomes ______.",a:"T\u2082 = \u221A2 T",s:`VP\xB2 = c gives P = \u221A(c/V).
Ideal gas: T \u221D PV = \u221A(cV), so T \u221D \u221AV.
When V becomes 2V, T becomes \u221A2 times.
T\u2082 = \u221A2 T.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:`The helium and argon are put in the flask at the same room temperature (300 K). The ratio of average kinetic energies (per molecule) of helium and argon is
(Give : Molar mass of helium = 4 g/mol, Molar mass of argon = 40 g/mol)`,o:["10 : 1","1 : 1","1 : 10","1 : \u221A10"],c:1,s:`Average translational kinetic energy per molecule = (3/2)k_BT.
It depends only on temperature, not on the mass of the gas.
Both are at 300 K, so the ratio is 1 : 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"For a diatomic gas, if \u03B3\u2081 = (C_P/C_V) for rigid molecules and \u03B3\u2082 = (C_P/C_V) for another diatomic molecules, but also having vibrational modes. Then, which one of the following options is correct? (C_P and C_V are specific heats of the gas at constant pressure and volume)",o:["\u03B3\u2082 = \u03B3\u2081","2\u03B3\u2082 = \u03B3\u2081","\u03B3\u2082 < \u03B3\u2081","\u03B3\u2082 > \u03B3\u2081"],c:2,s:`Rigid diatomic: f = 5, \u03B3\u2081 = 1 + 2/5 = 7/5 = 1.4.
With vibration, f = 7, \u03B3\u2082 = 1 + 2/7 = 9/7 \u2248 1.29.
More degrees of freedom lowers \u03B3, so \u03B3\u2082 < \u03B3\u2081.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The kinetic energy of translation of the molecules in 50 g of CO\u2082 gas at 17\xB0C is",o:["3986.3 J","3582.7 J","4102.8 J","4205.5 J"],c:2,s:`Moles n = 50/44 = 1.136 mol (M of CO\u2082 = 44 g/mol), T = 290 K.
Translational KE = (3/2)nRT.
= 1.5 \xD7 1.136 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 290 \u2248 4102.8 J (with R = 8.3 J/mol K).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"The translational degrees of freedom (f_t) and rotational degrees of freedom (f_r) of CH\u2084 molecule are",o:["f_t = 2 and f_r = 2","f_t = 3 and f_r = 2","f_t = 2 and f_r = 3","f_t = 3 and f_r = 3"],c:3,s:`CH\u2084 is a non-linear polyatomic molecule (tetrahedral).
Every free molecule has 3 translational degrees of freedom.
A non-linear molecule can rotate about 3 independent axes, so it has 3 rotational degrees of freedom.
So f_t = 3 and f_r = 3 (total 6, ignoring vibration).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"The specific heat at constant pressure of a real gas obeying PV\xB2 = RT equation is",o:["{R;3} + C_V","C_V + {R;2V}","R","C_V + R"],c:1,s:`From the first law at constant pressure: C_P = C_V + P(dV/dT)_P.
The gas obeys PV\xB2 = RT, so V\xB2 = RT/P.
At constant P, differentiate: 2V dV = (R/P) dT, so (dV/dT)_P = R/(2PV).
Then P(dV/dT)_P = R/(2V).
Hence C_P = C_V + R/(2V).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"Energy of 10 non rigid diatomic molecules at temperature T is",o:["35 RT","70 K_BT","{7;2}RT","35 K_BT"],c:3,s:`A non-rigid diatomic molecule has 3 translational + 2 rotational + 1 vibrational mode.
A vibrational mode has kinetic and potential energy, so it counts as 2 degrees of freedom.
Total f = 3 + 2 + 2 = 7.
Energy per molecule = {7;2} K_BT.
For 10 molecules: U = 10 \xD7 {7;2} K_BT = 35 K_BT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"A mixture of one mole of monoatomic gas and one mole of a diatomic gas (rigid) are kept at room temperature (27\xB0C). The ratio of specific heat of gases at constant volume respectively is",o:["{3;2}","{5;3}","{7;5}","{3;5}"],c:3,s:`Molar specific heat at constant volume is C_V = (f/2)R.
Monoatomic gas: f = 3, so C_V = {3;2}R.
Rigid diatomic gas: f = 5, so C_V = {5;2}R.
Ratio (monoatomic : diatomic) = (3/2)/(5/2) = {3;5}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The temperature of a gas is \u221278\xB0C and the average translational kinetic energy of its molecules is K. The temperature at which the average translational kinetic energy of the molecules of the same gas becomes 2K is",o:["127\xB0C","\u221239\xB0C","117\xB0C","\u221278\xB0C"],c:2,s:`Average translational KE = {3;2}k_BT, so it is proportional to the absolute temperature.
Initial T = \u221278 + 273 = 195 K.
To double the KE, double T: T' = 2 \xD7 195 = 390 K.
In \xB0C: 390 \u2212 273 = 117\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Two moles of a monoatomic gas is mixed with six moles of a diatomic gas. The molar specific heat of the mixture at constant volume is",o:["{3;2}R","{5;2}R","{9;4}R","{7;4}R"],c:2,s:`Take the diatomic gas as rigid (C_V = {5;2}R) and the monoatomic gas C_V = {3;2}R.
For a mixture, C_V = (n\u2081C_V1 + n\u2082C_V2)/(n\u2081 + n\u2082).
C_V = (2 \xD7 {3;2}R + 6 \xD7 {5;2}R)/8 = (3R + 15R)/8 = {18;8}R.
So C_V = {9;4}R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"A diatomic gas (\u03B3 = 1.4) does 200 J of work when it is expanded isobarically. The heat given to the gas in the process is",o:["800 J","600 J","700 J","850 J"],c:2,s:`In an isobaric process, W = nR\u0394T and Q = nC_P\u0394T.
So Q/W = C_P/R. With C_P = \u03B3R/(\u03B3 \u2212 1): Q/W = \u03B3/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = 1.4/0.4 = 3.5.
Q = 3.5 \xD7 200 = 700 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:`The average kinetic energy of a monoatomic molecule is 0.414 eV at temperature :
(Use k_B = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J/mol-K)`,o:["3200 K","3000 K","1500 K","1600 K"],c:0,s:`Average KE of a monoatomic molecule = {3;2}k_BT.
Convert: 0.414 eV = 0.414 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J = 6.62 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 J.
T = {2 \xD7 6.62 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070;3 \xD7 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3} = {1.325 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079;4.14 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3}.
T \u2248 3200 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:`The total kinetic energy of 1 mole of oxygen at 27\xB0C is
[Use universal gas constant (R) = 8.31 J/mole K]`,o:["5670.5 J","6845.5 J","5942.0 J","6232.5 J"],c:3,s:`Oxygen is diatomic with 3 translational + 2 rotational degrees of freedom, so f = 5.
At ordinary temperature the vibrational modes are not excited.
Total kinetic energy of 1 mole = {5;2}RT.
T = 27 + 273 = 300 K.
KE = 2.5 \xD7 8.31 \xD7 300 = 6232.5 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"N moles of a polyatomic gas (f = 6) must be mixed with two moles of a monoatomic gas so that the mixture behaves as a diatomic gas. The value of N is",o:["4","2","3","6"],c:0,s:`A diatomic gas has f = 5, so the mixture must have an effective f = 5.
For a mixture, the effective f is the mole-weighted average (same \u0394T, energy adds): f = (N \xD7 6 + 2 \xD7 3)/(N + 2).
Set it equal to 5: 6N + 6 = 5N + 10.
So N = 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"If three moles of monoatomic gas (\u03B3 = {5;3}) is mixed with two moles of a diatomic gas (\u03B3 = {7;5}), the value of adiabatic exponent \u03B3 for the mixture is",o:["1.40","1.35","1.52","1.75"],c:2,s:`Monoatomic: C_V = 1.5R. Diatomic: C_V = 2.5R.
Mixture C_V = (3 \xD7 1.5R + 2 \xD7 2.5R)/5 = (4.5 + 5)R/5 = 1.9R.
C_P = C_V + R = 2.9R.
\u03B3 = 2.9/1.9 \u2248 1.52.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The parameter that remains the same for molecules of all gases at a given temperature is",o:["kinetic energy","speed","momentum","mass"],c:0,s:`By kinetic theory the average translational KE of a molecule is {3;2}k_BT.
It depends only on temperature, not on the type of gas.
Speed, momentum and mass all depend on the molecular mass, so they differ from gas to gas.
So the common quantity is the (average) kinetic energy.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"A gas mixture consists of 8 moles of argon and 6 moles of oxygen at temperature T. Neglecting all vibrational modes, the total internal energy of the system is",o:["20 RT","29 RT","27 RT","21 RT"],c:2,s:`Argon is monoatomic: U = {3;2}RT per mole.
Oxygen is diatomic (no vibration): U = {5;2}RT per mole.
U = 8 \xD7 {3;2}RT + 6 \xD7 {5;2}RT = 12RT + 15RT.
U = 27 RT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The temperature at which the kinetic energy of oxygen molecules becomes double than its value at 27\xB0C is",o:["927\xB0C","1227\xB0C","327\xB0C","627\xB0C"],c:2,s:`Average KE of a molecule is proportional to the absolute temperature.
Initial T = 27 + 273 = 300 K.
For double KE, T' = 600 K.
In \xB0C: 600 \u2212 273 = 327\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"A gas mixture consists of 2 moles of oxygen and 4 moles of neon at temperature T. Neglecting all vibrational modes, the total internal energy of the system will be",o:["4RT","11RT","8RT","16RT"],c:1,s:`Oxygen (diatomic, no vibration): U = {5;2}RT per mole.
Neon (monoatomic): U = {3;2}RT per mole.
U = 2 \xD7 {5;2}RT + 4 \xD7 {3;2}RT = 5RT + 6RT.
U = 11 RT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:`A flask contains Hydrogen and Argon in the ratio 2 : 1 by mass. The temperature of the mixture is 30\xB0C. The ratio of average kinetic energy per molecule of the two gases (K_argon/K_hydrogen) is
(Given : Atomic Weight of Ar = 39.9)`,o:["{39.9;2}","39.9","1","2"],c:2,s:`Both gases are in the same mixture, so they are at the same temperature.
Average translational KE per molecule is {3;2}k_BT, independent of the mass of the molecule and of the mass ratio.
So K_argon = K_hydrogen and the ratio is 1.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The average kinetic energy of a molecule of the gas is",o:["proportional to absolute temperature","proportional to volume","dependent on the nature of the gas","proportional to pressure"],c:0,s:`From kinetic theory, the average translational KE per molecule = {3;2}k_BT.
It depends only on the absolute temperature T.
It does not depend on the volume, pressure or the nature of the gas.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Let \u03B3\u2081 be the ratio of molar specific heat at constant pressure and molar specific heat at constant volume of a monoatomic gas and \u03B3\u2082 be the similar ratio of diatomic gas. Considering the diatomic gas molecule as a rigid rotator, the ratio {\u03B3\u2081;\u03B3\u2082} is",o:["{21;25}","{25;21}","{35;27}","{27;35}"],c:1,s:`\u03B3 = 1 + 2/f.
Monoatomic: f = 3, so \u03B3\u2081 = 5/3.
Rigid diatomic: f = 5, so \u03B3\u2082 = 7/5.
\u03B3\u2081/\u03B3\u2082 = (5/3) \xD7 (5/7) = {25;21}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"According to law of equipartition of energy the molar specific heat of a diatomic gas at constant volume where the molecule has one additional vibration mode is",o:["{3;2}R","{7;2}R","{9;2}R","{5;2}R"],c:1,s:`A diatomic molecule has 3 translational + 2 rotational degrees of freedom.
One vibration mode adds 2 more (kinetic and potential energy), so f = 7.
Each degree of freedom contributes {1;2}R to C_V.
C_V = {7;2}R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"A flask contains hydrogen and oxygen in the ratio of 2 : 1 by mass at temperature 27\xB0C. The ratio of average kinetic energy per molecule of hydrogen and oxygen respectively is",o:["2 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 1","4 : 1"],c:2,s:`Both gases are at the same temperature (27\xB0C).
Average translational KE per molecule = {3;2}k_BT depends only on T.
So the ratio of KE per molecule is 1 : 1, whatever the mass ratio.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"The correct relation between \u03B3 = {C_p;C_v} and temperature T is",o:["\u03B3 \u221D T\u2070","\u03B3 \u221D {1;T}","\u03B3 \u221D T","\u03B3 \u221D {1;\u221AT}"],c:0,s:`For an ideal gas, \u03B3 = 1 + 2/f, where f is the number of degrees of freedom.
f depends only on the type of molecule (when the vibration modes are not excited), not on T.
So \u03B3 is constant: \u03B3 \u221D T\u2070.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"An ideal gas is in thermodynamic equilibrium. The number of degrees of freedom of a molecule of the gas is n. The internal energy of one mole of the gas is U_n, and the speed of sound in the gas is v_n. At a fixed temperature and pressure, which of the following is the correct option?",o:["v\u2083 < v\u2086 and U\u2083 > U\u2086","v\u2085 > v\u2083 and U\u2083 > U\u2085","v\u2085 > v\u2087 and U\u2085 < U\u2087","v\u2086 < v\u2087 and U\u2086 < U\u2087"],c:2,s:`Internal energy of one mole: U_n = {n;2}RT. At fixed T it increases with n, so U\u2083 < U\u2085 < U\u2086 < U\u2087.
Speed of sound v = \u221A(\u03B3RT/M) with \u03B3 = 1 + 2/n.
\u03B3 decreases as n increases, so v decreases as n increases: v\u2083 > v\u2085 > v\u2086 > v\u2087.
Check options: (a) v\u2083 < v\u2086 is false. (b) v\u2085 > v\u2083 is false. (c) v\u2085 > v\u2087 and U\u2085 < U\u2087 are both true. (d) v\u2086 < v\u2087 is false.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Following statements are given :
(A) The average kinetic energy of a gas molecule decreases when the temperature is reduced.
(B) The average kinetic energy of a gas molecule increases with increase in pressure at constant temperature.
(C) The average kinetic energy of a gas molecule decreases with increase in volume.
(D) Pressure of a gas increases with increase in temperature at constant pressure.
(E) The volume of gas decreases with increase in temperature.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A) and (D) only.","(A), (B) and (D) only.","(B) and (D) only.","(A), (B) and (E) only."],c:0,s:`Average KE of a molecule = {3;2}k_BT depends only on T.
(A) is true: lower T means lower KE.
(B) and (C) are false: KE does not change with pressure or volume at fixed T.
(E) is false: at constant pressure, V increases with T.
(D) is the statement meant to be true (pressure rises with temperature; this holds at constant volume, P \u221D T). The printed wording says constant pressure, but of the options only (A) and (D) fits, as in the key.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:`7 mole of certain monoatomic ideal gas undergoes a temperature increase of 40 K at constant pressure. The increase in the internal energy of the gas in this process is
(Given R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["5810 J","3486 J","11620 J","6972 J"],c:1,s:`Internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature, so \u0394U = nC_V\u0394T for any process.
Monoatomic: C_V = {3;2}R.
\u0394U = 7 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 40.
\u0394U = 3486 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"A gas has n degrees of freedom. The ratio of specific heat of gas at constant volume to the specific heat of gas at constant pressure will be",o:["{n;n+2}","{n+2;n}","{n;2n+2}","{n;n\u22122}"],c:0,s:`C_V = {n;2}R.
C_P = C_V + R = {n+2;2}R.
C_V/C_P = {n;2} \xF7 {n+2;2} = {n;n+2}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Which statements are correct about degrees of freedom?
(A) A molecule with n degrees of freedom has n\xB2 different ways of storing energy.
(B) Each degree of freedom is associated with {1;2}RT average energy per mole.
(C) A monoatomic gas molecule has 1 rotational degree of freedom where as diatomic molecule has 2 rotational degrees of freedom.
(D) CH\u2084 has a total of 6 degrees of freedom.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(B) and (C) only","(B) and (D) only","(A) and (B) only","(C) and (D) only"],c:1,s:`(A) false: n degrees of freedom means n ways of storing energy, not n\xB2.
(B) true: by equipartition, each degree of freedom carries {1;2}k_BT per molecule, i.e. {1;2}RT per mole.
(C) false: a monoatomic molecule has no rotational degree of freedom.
(D) true: CH\u2084 has 3 translational + 3 rotational = 6.
So (B) and (D) only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`A vessel contains 14 g of nitrogen gas at a temperature of 27\xB0C. The amount of heat to be transferred to the gas to double the r.m.s speed of its molecules will be
(Take R = 8.32 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9.)`,o:["2229 J","5616 J","9360 J","13,104 J"],c:2,s:`Moles of N\u2082 = 14/28 = 0.5 mol. Volume is fixed, so Q = nC_V\u0394T with C_V = {5;2}R.
v_rms \u221D \u221AT. Doubling v_rms needs T to become 4 times: T' = 4 \xD7 300 = 1200 K.
\u0394T = 900 K.
Q = 0.5 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 8.32 \xD7 900 = 9360 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"The ratio of specific heats {C_P;C_V} in terms of degree of freedom (f) is given by",o:["(1 + {f;3})","(1 + {2;f})","(1 + {f;2})","(1 + {1;f})"],c:1,s:`C_V = {f;2}R and C_P = C_V + R = ({f;2} + 1)R.
\u03B3 = C_P/C_V = ({f;2} + 1) \xF7 {f;2} = 1 + {2;f}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"A thermally insulated vessel contains an ideal gas of molecular mass M and ratio of specific heats 1.4. Vessel is moving with speed v and is suddenly brought to rest. Assuming no heat is lost to the surrounding and vessel temperature of the gas increases by (R = universal gas constant)",o:["{Mv\xB2;7R}","{Mv\xB2;5R}","2{Mv\xB2;7R}","7{Mv\xB2;5R}"],c:1,s:`For n moles, mass = nM and the kinetic energy lost = {1;2}nMv\xB2.
This goes into internal energy of the gas: {1;2}nMv\xB2 = nC_V\u0394T.
\u03B3 = 1.4 gives C_V = R/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = R/0.4 = {5;2}R.
\u0394T = {Mv\xB2;2C_V} = {Mv\xB2;5R}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"A flask contains argon and oxygen in the ratio of 3 : 2 by mass and the mixture is kept at 27\xB0C. The ratio of their average kinetic energy per molecule respectively will be",o:["3 : 2","9 : 4","2 : 3","1 : 1"],c:3,s:`Both gases are at the same temperature.
Average KE per molecule = {3;2}k_BT (translational) is the same for all ideal gases at the same T.
So the ratio is 1 : 1, irrespective of the mass ratio.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`According to kinetic theory of gases,
A. The motion of the gas molecules freezes at 0\xB0C.
B. The mean free path of gas molecules decreases if the density of molecules is increased.
C. The mean free path of gas molecules increases if temperature is increased keeping pressure constant.
D. Average kinetic energy per molecule per degree of freedom is {3;2}k_BT (for monoatomic gases).
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["A and C only","B and C only","A and B only","C and D only"],c:1,s:`A is false: molecular motion stops only near absolute zero (0 K), not at 0\xB0C.
B is true: \u03BB = {1;\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n}, so \u03BB decreases when the number density n increases.
C is true: \u03BB = {k_BT;\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 P}, so at constant P it increases with T.
D is false: per degree of freedom the energy is {1;2}k_BT; {3;2}k_BT is the total for a monoatomic molecule.
So B and C only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`A cylinder of fixed capacity of 44.8 litres contains helium gas at standard temperature and pressure. The amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of gas in the cylinder by 20.0\xB0C will be
(Given gas constant R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9)`,o:["249 J","415 J","498 J","830 J"],c:2,s:`At STP 1 mol occupies 22.4 L, so n = 44.8/22.4 = 2 mol.
The volume is fixed, so Q = nC_V\u0394T with C_V = {3;2}R for helium.
Q = 2 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 20.
Q = 498 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"The rms speeds of the molecules of Hydrogen, Oxygen and Carbon-dioxide at the same temperature are v_H, v_O and v_C respectively then",o:["v_C > v_O > v_H","v_H = v_O > v_C","v_H > v_O > v_C","v_H = v_O = v_C"],c:2,s:`v_rms = \u221A{3RT;M}, so at the same T it is inversely proportional to \u221AM.
Molar masses: H\u2082 = 2, O\u2082 = 32, CO\u2082 = 44.
The lighter the gas, the faster its molecules: v_H > v_O > v_C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"If the rms speed of oxygen molecules at 0\xB0C is 160 m/s. Find the rms speed of hydrogen molecules at 0\xB0C.",o:["40 m/s","80 m/s","640 m/s","332 m/s"],c:2,s:`At the same temperature, v_rms \u221D {1;\u221AM}.
v_H/v_O = \u221A(M_O/M_H) = \u221A(32/2) = 4.
v_H = 4 \xD7 160 = 640 m/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"The amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of 4 moles of a rigid diatomic gas from 0\xB0C to 50\xB0C when no work is done is ______. (R is the universal gas constant)",o:["750R","250R","500R","175R"],c:2,s:`No work is done, so the volume is constant and Q = nC_V\u0394T.
Rigid diatomic gas: C_V = {5;2}R.
\u0394T = 50 K.
Q = 4 \xD7 {5;2}R \xD7 50 = 500R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"The correct relation between the degrees of freedom f and the ratio of specific heat \u03B3 is",o:["f = {2;\u03B3+1}","f = {1;\u03B3+1}","f = {2;\u03B3\u22121}","f = {\u03B3+1;2}"],c:2,s:`\u03B3 = 1 + {2;f}.
So \u03B3 \u2212 1 = {2;f}.
Hence f = {2;\u03B3\u22121}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqm",st:"Maxwell distribution, diffusion and real gases",q:"For a gas C_P \u2212 C_V = R in a state P and C_P \u2212 C_V = 1.10 R in a state Q. T_P and T_Q are the temperatures in two different states P and Q respectively. Then",o:["T_P = T_Q","T_P < T_Q","T_P > T_Q","T_P = 0.9T_Q"],c:2,s:`C_P \u2212 C_V = R holds exactly only for an ideal gas.
For a real gas, C_P \u2212 C_V is larger than R because extra work is done against the attraction between molecules during expansion.
This deviation is larger at lower temperature, and the gas behaves more ideally at higher temperature.
State P (difference = R) is the more ideal one, so it is at the higher temperature: T_P > T_Q.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"What will be the average value of energy for a monoatomic gas in thermal equilibrium at temperature T ?",o:["k_BT","{3;2}k_BT","{1;2}k_BT","{2;3}k_BT"],c:1,s:`A monoatomic molecule has 3 translational degrees of freedom.
Each carries {1;2}k_BT on average (equipartition).
Average energy per molecule = 3 \xD7 {1;2}k_BT = {3;2}k_BT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"A polyatomic ideal gas has 24 vibrational modes. What is the value of \u03B3 ?",o:["10.3","1.03","1.30","1.37"],c:1,s:`Each vibrational mode counts as 2 degrees of freedom (kinetic and potential).
f = 3 (translational) + 3 (rotational) + 2 \xD7 24 = 54.
\u03B3 = 1 + {2;f} = 1 + {2;54} = 1.037.
This is about 1.03 (option b). The other options are far off.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"Two ideal polyatomic gases at temperature T\u2081 and T\u2082 are mixed so that there is no loss of energy. If F\u2081 and F\u2082, m\u2081 and m\u2082, n\u2081 and n\u2082 be the degree of freedom, masses, number of molecules of the first and second gases respectively, the temperature of mixture of these two gases is",o:["{n\u2081F\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082T\u2082;n\u2081F\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082}","{n\u2081F\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082T\u2082;F\u2081 + F\u2082}","{n\u2081F\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082T\u2082;n\u2081 + n\u2082}","{n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082;n\u2081 + n\u2082}"],c:0,s:`Energy of one molecule is {F;2}k_BT, so the total energy of gas 1 is {n\u2081F\u2081;2}k_BT\u2081 and of gas 2 is {n\u2082F\u2082;2}k_BT\u2082.
No energy is lost, so the total energy is conserved.
After mixing, total energy = {n\u2081F\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082;2}k_BT.
Equate: (n\u2081F\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082)T = n\u2081F\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082T\u2082.
T = {n\u2081F\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082T\u2082;n\u2081F\u2081 + n\u2082F\u2082}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"If one mole of the polyatomic gas is having two vibrational modes and \u03B2 is the ratio of molar specific heats for polyatomic gas (\u03B2 = {C_P;C_V}) then the value of \u03B2 is",o:["1.2","1.02","1.25","1.35"],c:0,s:`A non-linear polyatomic molecule has 3 translational + 3 rotational degrees of freedom.
Each vibrational mode adds 2, so two modes add 4: f = 3 + 3 + 4 = 10.
\u03B2 = 1 + {2;f} = 1 + {2;10} = 1.2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"What will be the average value of energy along one degree of freedom for an ideal gas in thermal equilibrium at a temperature T ? (k_B is Boltzmann constant)",o:["{2;3}k_BT","{3;2}k_BT","{1;2}k_BT","k_BT"],c:2,s:`The law of equipartition of energy says that every degree of freedom carries an average energy of {1;2}k_BT per molecule.
So the energy along one degree of freedom is {1;2}k_BT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"An ideal gas in a cylinder is separated by a piston in such a way that the entropy of one part is S\u2081 and that of the other part is S\u2082. Given that S\u2081 > S\u2082. If the piston is removed then the total entropy of the system will be",o:["S\u2081 \u2212 S\u2082","S\u2081/S\u2082","S\u2081 + S\u2082","S\u2081 \xD7 S\u2082"],c:2,s:`Entropy is an extensive quantity: the entropy of a system is the sum of the entropies of its parts.
The question treats the two parts as one system with no extra irreversible change counted.
So the total entropy = S\u2081 + S\u2082.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"A diatomic gas having C_P = {7;2}R and C_V = {5;2}R, is heated at constant pressure. The ratio dU : dQ : dW is",o:["3 : 7 : 2","5 : 7 : 2","3 : 5 : 2","5 : 7 : 3"],c:1,s:`At constant pressure: dU = nC_VdT, dQ = nC_PdT, dW = nRdT.
Ratio = C_V : C_P : R = {5;2}R : {7;2}R : R.
Multiply by 2/R: 5 : 7 : 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"A gas mixture consists of 3 moles of oxygen and 5 moles of argon at temperature T. Assuming the gases to be ideal and the oxygen bond to be rigid, the total internal energy (in units of RT) of the mixture is",o:["15","13","20","11"],c:0,s:`Rigid oxygen (diatomic): U = {5;2}RT per mole. Argon (monoatomic): U = {3;2}RT per mole.
U = 3 \xD7 {5;2}RT + 5 \xD7 {3;2}RT = 7.5RT + 7.5RT.
U = 15 RT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"Consider a gas of triatomic molecules. The molecules are assumed to be triangular and made of massless rigid rods whose vertices are occupied by atoms. The internal energy of a mole of the gas at temperature T is",o:["{5;2}RT","{3;2}RT","{9;2}RT","3RT"],c:3,s:`A triangular (non-linear) rigid molecule has 3 translational and 3 rotational degrees of freedom.
The rods are rigid, so there is no vibration: f = 6.
U = {f;2}RT = {6;2}RT = 3RT per mole.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"To raise the temperature of a certain mass of gas by 50\xB0C at a constant pressure, 160 calories of heat is required. When the same mass of gas is cooled by 100\xB0C at constant volume, 240 calories of heat is released. How many degrees of freedom does each molecule of this gas have (assume gas to be ideal)?",o:["5","6","3","7"],c:1,s:`At constant pressure: nC\u209A(50) = 160, so nC\u209A = 3.2 cal/K.
At constant volume: nC\u1D65(100) = 240, so nC\u1D65 = 2.4 cal/K.
\u03B3 = C\u209A/C\u1D65 = 3.2/2.4 = 4/3.
\u03B3 = 1 + 2/f, so 2/f = 1/3 and f = 6.
Answer: 6 degrees of freedom.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"Number of molecules in a volume of 4 cm\xB3 of a perfect monoatomic gas at some temperature T and at a pressure of 2 cm of mercury is close to? (Given, mean kinetic energy of a molecule (at T) is 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 erg, g = 980 cm/s\xB2, density of mercury = 13.6 g/cm\xB3)",o:["4.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078","4.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","5.8 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076","5.8 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078"],c:0,s:`Mean kinetic energy of a molecule E = (3/2)kT, so kT = 2E/3.
Pressure P = \u03C1gh = 13.6 \xD7 980 \xD7 2 = 26656 dyne/cm\xB2.
PV = NkT = N(2E/3), so N = 3PV/(2E).
N = 3 \xD7 26656 \xD7 4 / (2 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074) = 4.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078.
Answer: 4.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2078.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"Molecules of an ideal gas are known to have three translational degrees of freedom and two rotational degrees of freedom. The gas is maintained at a temperature of T. The total internal energy, U of a mole of this gas, and the value of \u03B3 (= C\u209A/C\u1D65) are given respectively, by",o:["U = {5;2}RT and \u03B3 = {6;5}","U = 5RT and \u03B3 = {7;5}","U = {5;2}RT and \u03B3 = {7;5}","U = 5RT and \u03B3 = {6;5}"],c:2,s:`Total degrees of freedom f = 3 + 2 = 5.
Internal energy of one mole U = (f/2)RT = (5/2)RT.
\u03B3 = 1 + 2/f = 1 + 2/5 = 7/5.
Answer: U = (5/2)RT and \u03B3 = 7/5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Two moles of an ideal gas with C\u209A/C\u1D65 = {5;3} are mixed with 3 moles of another ideal gas with C\u209A/C\u1D65 = {4;3}. The value of C\u209A/C\u1D65 for the mixture is",o:["1.50","1.45","1.47","1.42"],c:3,s:`Use C\u1D65 = R/(\u03B3 \u2212 1).
Gas 1: \u03B3 = 5/3, so C\u1D65 = 1.5R. Gas 2: \u03B3 = 4/3, so C\u1D65 = 3R.
Mixture C\u1D65 = (2 \xD7 1.5R + 3 \xD7 3R)/5 = 2.4R, so C\u209A = C\u1D65 + R = 3.4R.
\u03B3 = 3.4/2.4 = 1.4167, which is about 1.42.
Answer: 1.42.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Consider a mixture of n moles of helium gas and 2n moles of oxygen gas (molecules taken to be rigid) as an ideal gas. Its C\u209A/C\u1D65 value will be",o:["40/27","23/15","19/13","67/45"],c:2,s:`Helium: C\u1D65 = 3R/2, C\u209A = 5R/2. Rigid oxygen: C\u1D65 = 5R/2, C\u209A = 7R/2.
Mixture C\u1D65 = (n \xD7 3R/2 + 2n \xD7 5R/2)/(3n) = (13R/2)/3 = 13R/6.
Mixture C\u209A = (n \xD7 5R/2 + 2n \xD7 7R/2)/(3n) = (19R/2)/3 = 19R/6.
\u03B3 = C\u209A/C\u1D65 = 19/13.
Answer: 19/13.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Consider two ideal diatomic gases A and B at some temperature T. Molecules of the gas A are rigid, and have a mass m. Molecules of the gas B have an additional vibrational mode, and have a mass m/4. The ratio of the specific heats (C\u1D65 of A and C\u1D65 of B) of gas A and B, respectively is",o:["5 : 9","7 : 9","3 : 5","5 : 7"],c:3,s:`Rigid diatomic gas A: f = 5 (3 translational + 2 rotational), so C\u1D65 = (5/2)R.
Gas B has one extra vibrational mode, which counts 2 degrees of freedom: f = 7, so C\u1D65 = (7/2)R.
The mass of the molecule does not change C\u1D65.
Ratio C\u1D65(A) : C\u1D65(B) = 5 : 7.
Answer: 5 : 7.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"The specific heats, C\u209A and C\u1D65 of a gas of diatomic molecules, A, are given (in units of J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9) by 29 and 22, respectively. Another gas of diatomic molecules, B, has the corresponding values 30 and 21. If they are treated as ideal gases, then",o:["A has a vibrational mode but B has none.","A has one vibrational mode and B has two.","A is rigid but B has a vibrational mode.","Both A and B have a vibrational mode each."],c:0,s:`For an ideal gas \u03B3 = C\u209A/C\u1D65 = 1 + 2/f, so f = 2/(\u03B3 \u2212 1).
Gas A: \u03B3 = 29/22 = 1.32, so f = 2/0.32, which is about 6.
Gas B: \u03B3 = 30/21 = 1.43, so f = 2/0.43, which is about 5.
A rigid diatomic molecule has f = 5, so B is rigid with no vibration.
A has more than 5 degrees of freedom, so it has a vibrational mode.
Answer: A has a vibrational mode but B has none.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`A cylinder with fixed capacity of 67.2 litre contains helium gas at STP. The amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of the gas by 20\xB0C is
[Given that R = 8.31 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9]`,o:["350 J","700 J","748 J","374 J"],c:2,s:`At STP one mole occupies 22.4 L, so n = 67.2/22.4 = 3 mol.
The volume is fixed, so Q = nC\u1D65\u0394T with C\u1D65 = 3R/2 for helium.
Q = 3 \xD7 (3/2) \xD7 8.31 \xD7 20 = 747.9 J.
Answer: about 748 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"When heat Q is supplied to a diatomic gas of rigid molecules, at constant volume its temperature increases by \u0394T. The heat required to produce the same change in temperature, at a constant pressure is",o:["{2;3}Q","{3;2}Q","{7;5}Q","{5;3}Q"],c:2,s:`Rigid diatomic gas: C\u1D65 = 5R/2 and C\u209A = 7R/2.
At constant volume Q = nC\u1D65\u0394T = n(5R/2)\u0394T.
At constant pressure Q\u2032 = nC\u209A\u0394T = n(7R/2)\u0394T.
Q\u2032/Q = C\u209A/C\u1D65 = 7/5.
Answer: Q\u2032 = (7/5)Q.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Two moles of helium gas is mixed with three moles of hydrogen molecules (taken to be rigid). What is the molar specific heat of mixture at constant volume? (R = 8.3 J/mol K)",o:["21.6 J/mol K","19.7 J/mol K","15.7 J/mol K","17.4 J/mol K"],c:3,s:`Helium: C\u1D65 = 3R/2. Rigid hydrogen: C\u1D65 = 5R/2.
Mixture C\u1D65 = (2 \xD7 3R/2 + 3 \xD7 5R/2)/(2 + 3) = (3R + 7.5R)/5 = 2.1R.
2.1 \xD7 8.3 = 17.4 J/mol K.
Answer: 17.4 J/mol K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`A 15 g mass of nitrogen gas is enclosed in a vessel at a temperature 27\xB0C. Amount of heat transferred to the gas, so that rms velocity of molecules is doubled, is about
[Take R = 8.3 J/K mole]`,o:["14 kJ","10 kJ","0.9 kJ","6 kJ"],c:1,s:`vrms is proportional to \u221AT, so doubling vrms makes T four times: 300 K to 1200 K, \u0394T = 900 K.
The vessel is rigid, so Q = nC\u1D65\u0394T with n = 15/28 and C\u1D65 = 5R/2.
Q = (15/28) \xD7 (5/2) \xD7 8.3 \xD7 900 \u2248 10004 J.
Answer: about 10 kJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"A gas mixture consists of 3 moles of oxygen and 5 moles of argon at temperature T. Considering only translational and rotational modes, the total internal energy of the system is",o:["20 RT","12 RT","4 RT","15 RT"],c:3,s:`Oxygen (diatomic): 3 translational + 2 rotational, so f = 5 and U = (5/2)RT per mole.
Argon (monoatomic): f = 3 and U = (3/2)RT per mole.
Total U = 3 \xD7 (5/2)RT + 5 \xD7 (3/2)RT = 7.5RT + 7.5RT = 15RT.
Answer: 15RT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Two moles of helium are mixed with n moles of hydrogen. If C\u209A/C\u1D65 = {3;2} for the mixture, then the value of n is",o:["1","2","3","{3;2}"],c:1,s:`Helium: C\u1D65 = 1.5R, C\u209A = 2.5R. Hydrogen: C\u1D65 = 2.5R, C\u209A = 3.5R.
Mixture C\u209A/C\u1D65 = (2 \xD7 2.5 + n \xD7 3.5)/(2 \xD7 1.5 + n \xD7 2.5) = 3/2.
2(5 + 3.5n) = 3(3 + 2.5n), so 10 + 7n = 9 + 7.5n.
0.5n = 1, so n = 2.
Answer: n = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:`C\u209A and C\u1D65 are specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume respectively. It is observed that
C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = a for hydrogen gas
C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = b for nitrogen gas
The correct relation between a and b is`,o:["a = {1;14}b","a = b","a = 14b","a = 28b"],c:2,s:`Specific heats here are per unit mass, so C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R/M (M = molar mass).
Hydrogen: a = R/2. Nitrogen: b = R/28.
a/b = 28/2 = 14.
Answer: a = 14b.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"An ideal gas has molecules with 5 degrees of freedom. The ratio of specific heats at constant pressure (C\u209A) and at constant volume (C\u1D65) is",o:["6","{7;2}","{5;2}","{7;5}"],c:3,s:`For f degrees of freedom C\u1D65 = (f/2)R and C\u209A = (f/2 + 1)R.
For f = 5: C\u1D65 = 5R/2 and C\u209A = 7R/2.
\u03B3 = C\u209A/C\u1D65 = 1 + 2/f = 7/5.
Answer: 7/5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"N moles of a diatomic gas in a cylinder are at a temperature T. Heat is supplied to the cylinder such that the temperature remains constant but n moles of the diatomic gas get converted into monoatomic gas. What is the change in the total kinetic energy of the gas?",o:["0","{5;2}nRT","{1;2}nRT","{3;2}nRT"],c:2,s:`Total kinetic energy (translation + rotation) of n moles of diatomic gas = n \xD7 (5/2)RT.
When these n moles become monoatomic, each molecule splits into 2 atoms, giving 2n moles of monoatomic gas.
Their kinetic energy = 2n \xD7 (3/2)RT = 3nRT.
Change = 3nRT \u2212 (5/2)nRT = (1/2)nRT.
Answer: (1/2)nRT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"Using equipartition of energy, the specific heat (in J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9) of aluminium at room temperature can be estimated to be (atomic weight of aluminium = 27)",o:["25","410","925","1850"],c:2,s:`In a solid each atom vibrates in 3 directions, giving 6 degrees of freedom (3 kinetic + 3 potential).
Molar specific heat = 6 \xD7 (R/2) = 3R \u2248 25 J/mol K.
Per kilogram: 25 / 0.027 kg \u2248 925 J/kg K.
Answer: about 925 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"A gas molecule of mass M at the surface of the Earth has kinetic energy equivalent to 0\xB0C. If it were to go up straight without colliding with any other molecules, how high it would rise? Assume that the height attained is much less than radius of the earth. (kB is Boltzmann constant)",o:["0","{273kB;2Mg}","{546kB;3Mg}","{819kB;2Mg}"],c:3,s:`Kinetic energy of a molecule at 0\xB0C (273 K) is (3/2)kB \xD7 273.
It rises until all this energy becomes potential energy: Mgh = (3/2)kB \xD7 273.
h = 3 \xD7 273 kB/(2Mg) = 819 kB/(2Mg).
Answer: h = 819 kB/(2Mg).`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"One kg of a diatomic gas is at a pressure of 8 \xD7 10\u2074 N/m\xB2. The density of the gas is 4 kg/m\xB3. What is the energy of the gas due to its thermal motion?",o:["3 \xD7 10\u2074 J","5 \xD7 10\u2074 J","6 \xD7 10\u2074 J","7 \xD7 10\u2074 J"],c:1,s:`Volume V = mass/density = 1/4 = 0.25 m\xB3.
Diatomic gas: f = 5, so U = (5/2)nRT = (5/2)PV.
U = 2.5 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 0.25 = 5 \xD7 10\u2074 J.
Answer: 5 \xD7 10\u2074 J.`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"If C\u209A and C\u1D65 denote the specific heats of nitrogen per unit mass at constant pressure and constant volume respectively, then",o:["C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = 28R","C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R/28","C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R/14","C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R"],c:1,s:`Molar specific heats satisfy C\u209A,m \u2212 C\u1D65,m = R.
Specific heats per unit mass are the molar ones divided by the molar mass M.
For nitrogen M = 28 g/mol, so C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R/28.
Answer: C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R/28.`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"Two rigid boxes containing different ideal gases are placed on a table. Box A contains one mole of nitrogen at temperature T\u2080, while Box B contains one mole of helium at temperature (7/3)T\u2080. The boxes are then put into thermal contact with each other and heat flows between them until the gases reach a common final temperature. (Ignore the heat capacity of boxes). Then, the final temperature of the gases, T_f, in terms of T\u2080 is",o:["T_f = {5;2}T\u2080","T_f = {3;7}T\u2080","T_f = {7;3}T\u2080","T_f = {3;2}T\u2080"],c:3,s:`The boxes are rigid, so no work is done and the total internal energy is conserved.
Nitrogen: C\u1D65 = 5R/2. Helium: C\u1D65 = 3R/2.
(5R/2)T\u2080 + (3R/2)(7T\u2080/3) = (5R/2 + 3R/2)T_f.
2.5T\u2080 + 3.5T\u2080 = 4T_f, so 6T\u2080 = 4T_f.
Answer: T_f = (3/2)T\u2080.`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"A gaseous mixture consists of 16 g of helium and 16 g of oxygen. The ratio C\u209A/C\u1D65 of the mixture is",o:["1.4","1.54","1.59","1.62"],c:3,s:`Moles: helium 16/4 = 4 mol; oxygen 16/32 = 0.5 mol.
C\u1D65 total = 4 \xD7 1.5R + 0.5 \xD7 2.5R = 7.25R.
C\u209A total = 4 \xD7 2.5R + 0.5 \xD7 3.5R = 11.75R.
\u03B3 = 11.75/7.25 = 1.62.
Answer: 1.62.`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"One mole of ideal monoatomic gas (\u03B3 = 5/3) is mixed with one mole of diatomic gas (\u03B3 = 7/5). What is \u03B3 for the mixture? \u03B3 denotes the ratio of specific heat at constant pressure, to that at constant volume.",o:["{3;2}","{23;15}","{35;23}","{4;3}"],c:0,s:`Monoatomic: C\u1D65 = 1.5R, C\u209A = 2.5R. Diatomic: C\u1D65 = 2.5R, C\u209A = 3.5R.
For 1 mole of each: C\u1D65 = (1.5 + 2.5)R/2 = 2R and C\u209A = (2.5 + 3.5)R/2 = 3R.
\u03B3 = 3R/2R = 3/2.
Answer: 3/2.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"1 mole of a gas with \u03B3 = 7/5 is mixed with 1 mole of a gas with \u03B3 = 5/3, then the value of \u03B3 for the resulting mixture is",o:["7/5","2/5","24/16","12/7"],c:2,s:`Use C\u1D65 = R/(\u03B3 \u2212 1).
Gas 1 (\u03B3 = 7/5): C\u1D65 = 2.5R. Gas 2 (\u03B3 = 5/3): C\u1D65 = 1.5R.
Mixture C\u1D65 = (2.5R + 1.5R)/2 = 2R, so C\u209A = 3R.
\u03B3 = 3/2 = 24/16.
Answer: 24/16 (= 3/2).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`A mixture of ideal gas containing 5 moles of monatomic gas and 1 mole of rigid diatomic gas is initially at pressure P\u2080, volume V\u2080, and temperature T\u2080. If the gas mixture is adiabatically compressed to a volume V\u2080/4, then the correct statement(s) is/are,
(Given 2^1.2 = 2.3; 2^3.2 = 9.2; R is gas constant)`,o:["The final pressure of the gas mixture after compression is in between 9P\u2080 and 10P\u2080.","The average kinetic energy of the gas mixture after compression is in between 18RT\u2080 and 19RT\u2080","Adiabatic constant of the gas mixture is 1.6","The work |W| done during the process is 13RT\u2080"],c:[0,2,3],s:`C\u1D65 of mixture = 5 \xD7 1.5R + 1 \xD7 2.5R = 10R (total) and C\u209A = 5 \xD7 2.5R + 3.5R = 16R, so \u03B3 = 16/10 = 1.6. (c) is correct.
PV^\u03B3 = constant: P = P\u2080(4)^1.6 = P\u2080 \xD7 2^3.2 = 9.2P\u2080, between 9P\u2080 and 10P\u2080. (a) is correct.
TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant: T = T\u2080(4)^0.6 = 2^1.2 T\u2080 = 2.3T\u2080.
Final total internal energy = 10R \xD7 2.3T\u2080 = 23RT\u2080, not 18 to 19RT\u2080. (b) is wrong.
Adiabatic: |W| = \u0394U = 10R(2.3T\u2080 \u2212 T\u2080) = 13RT\u2080. (d) is correct.
Answer: (a), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"One mole of a monatomic ideal gas goes through a thermodynamic cycle, as shown in the volume versus temperature (V-T) diagram. The correct statement(s) is/are (R is the gas constant)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"V-T diagram of a cycle: point 1 at (T0, V0), point 2 at (2T0, 2V0), point 3 at (T0, 2V0), point 4 at (T0/2, V0); the lines 1 to 2 and 3 to 4 are slanted, 2 to 3 and 4 to 1 are horizontal",d:[["l",60,170,320,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",325,174,"T",""],["t",56,16,"V","e"],["l",95,120,95,170,"d"],["l",130,60,130,170,"d"],["l",200,60,200,170,"d"],["l",60,120,95,120,"d"],["l",60,60,130,60,"d"],["l",95,170,95,175],["l",130,170,130,175],["l",165,170,165,175],["l",200,170,200,175],["t",95,188,"T\u2080/2","m","s"],["t",130,188,"T\u2080","m","s"],["t",165,188,"3T\u2080/2","m","s"],["t",200,188,"2T\u2080","m","s"],["t",54,124,"V\u2080","e"],["t",54,64,"2V\u2080","e"],["l",95,120,130,120,"a"],["l",130,120,200,60,"a"],["l",200,60,130,60,"a"],["l",130,60,95,120,"a"],["t",86,136,"4","m"],["t",138,134,"1",""],["t",124,52,"3","e"],["t",206,54,"2",""]]},o:["Work done in this thermodynamic cycle (1\u21922\u21923\u21924\u21921) is |W| = \xBDRT\u2080","The ratio of heat transfer during processes 1\u21922 and 2\u21923 is |Q\u2081\u2192\u2082/Q\u2082\u2192\u2083| = {5;3}","The above thermodynamic cycle exhibits only isochoric and adiabatic processes.","The ratio of heat transfer during processes 1\u21922 and 3\u21924 is |Q\u2081\u2192\u2082/Q\u2083\u2192\u2084| = {1;2}"],c:[0,1],s:`Read the diagram: 1 (T\u2080, V\u2080), 2 (2T\u2080, 2V\u2080), 3 (T\u2080, 2V\u2080), 4 (T\u2080/2, V\u2080).
Process 1\u21922: V/T is constant, so it is isobaric at P\u2081 = RT\u2080/V\u2080. Work = P\u2081V\u2080 = RT\u2080. Q\u2081\u2082 = C\u209A(T\u2080) = (5/2)RT\u2080.
Process 2\u21923 is isochoric: Q\u2082\u2083 = C\u1D65(\u2212T\u2080), magnitude (3/2)RT\u2080. Ratio 5/3, so (b) is true.
Process 3\u21924: V/T constant, so isobaric at P\u2081/2. Work = \u2212(P\u2081/2)V\u2080 = \u2212RT\u2080/2. Q\u2083\u2084 magnitude = C\u209A(T\u2080/2) = (5/4)RT\u2080, so |Q\u2081\u2082/Q\u2083\u2084| = 2, not 1/2: (d) is false.
Net work = RT\u2080 \u2212 RT\u2080/2 + 0 + 0 = RT\u2080/2: (a) is true.
Two isobaric steps are present, so (c) is false.
Answer: (a) and (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"An ideal monoatomic gas is confined in a horizontal cylinder by a spring loaded piston (as shown in the figure). Initially the gas is at temperature T\u2081, pressure P\u2081 and volume V\u2081 and the spring is in its relaxed state. The gas is then heated very slowly to temperature T\u2082, pressure P\u2082 and volume V\u2082. During this process the piston moves out by a distance x. Ignoring the friction between the piston and the cylinder, the correct statement(s) is(are)",fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"Horizontal cylinder with gas on the left of a movable piston and a spring joining the piston to the fixed wall on the right",d:[["l",20,40,262,40],["l",20,110,262,110],["l",20,40,20,110,"B"],["r",20,40,110,70,"f"],["t",75,80,"gas","m"],["l",130,40,130,110,"B"],["p","M130 75 H146 L153 61 L167 89 L181 61 L195 89 L209 61 L223 89 L230 75 H262"],["l",262,28,262,122,"B"],["l",262,40,272,30],["l",262,55,272,45],["l",262,70,272,60],["l",262,85,272,75],["l",262,100,272,90],["l",262,115,272,105],["t",196,100,"spring","ms"],["t",75,138,"P\u2081, V\u2081, T\u2081","m"]]},o:["If V\u2082 = 2V\u2081 and T\u2082 = 3T\u2081, then the energy stored in the spring is {1;4}P\u2081V\u2081","If V\u2082 = 2V\u2081 and T\u2082 = 3T\u2081, then the change in internal energy is 3P\u2081V\u2081","If V\u2082 = 3V\u2081 and T\u2082 = 4T\u2081, then the work done by the gas is {7;3}P\u2081V\u2081","If V\u2082 = 3V\u2081 and T\u2082 = 4T\u2081, then the heat supplied to the gas is {17;6}P\u2081V\u2081"],c:[0,1,2],s:`The spring is relaxed at the start, so the outside pressure balances P\u2081. Later P = P\u2081 + kx/A, which is linear in V, with x = (V \u2212 V\u2081)/A.
(a),(b): V\u2082 = 2V\u2081, T\u2082 = 3T\u2081 gives P\u2082V\u2082 = 3P\u2081V\u2081, so P\u2082 = 1.5P\u2081. Spring energy = \xBD(P\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081)(V\u2082 \u2212 V\u2081) = \xBD \xD7 0.5P\u2081 \xD7 V\u2081 = P\u2081V\u2081/4. \u0394U = (3/2)(P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081) = (3/2)(2P\u2081V\u2081) = 3P\u2081V\u2081. Both true.
(c): V\u2082 = 3V\u2081, T\u2082 = 4T\u2081 gives P\u2082 = 4P\u2081/3. Work = area under the P-V line = \xBD(P\u2081 + P\u2082)(2V\u2081) = (7/3)P\u2081V\u2081. True.
(d): \u0394U = (3/2)(4 \u2212 1)P\u2081V\u2081 = 4.5P\u2081V\u2081, so Q = 4.5P\u2081V\u2081 + (7/3)P\u2081V\u2081 = (41/6)P\u2081V\u2081, not 17/6. False.
Answer: (a), (b), (c).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"A container of fixed volume has a mixture of one mole of hydrogen and one mole of helium in equilibrium at temperature T. Assuming the gases are ideal, the correct statement(s) is(are)",o:["The average energy per mole of the gas mixture is 2RT.","The ratio of speed of sound in the gas mixture to that in helium gas is \u221A(6/5).","The ratio of the rms speed of helium atoms to that of hydrogen molecules is 1/2.","The ratio of the rms speed of helium atoms to that of hydrogen molecules is 1/\u221A2."],c:[0,1,3],s:`(a) Energy of the mixture = (5/2 + 3/2)RT = 4RT for 2 moles, so 2RT per mole. True.
(b) Mixture: C\u1D65 = 2R, C\u209A = 3R, \u03B3 = 1.5, M = (2 + 4)/2 = 3 g/mol. Helium: \u03B3 = 5/3, M = 4. Speed of sound is proportional to \u221A(\u03B3/M): ratio = \u221A[(1.5/3)/((5/3)/4)] = \u221A(6/5). True.
(c),(d) vrms is proportional to 1/\u221AM: He/H\u2082 = \u221A(2/4) = 1/\u221A2. So (c) is false and (d) is true.
Answer: (a), (b), (d).`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"C\u1D65 and C\u209A denote the molar specific heat capacities of a gas at constant volume and constant pressure, respectively. Then",o:["C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas.","C\u209A + C\u1D65 is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas.","C\u209A/C\u1D65 is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas.","C\u209A\xB7C\u1D65 is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas."],c:[1,3],s:`Monoatomic: C\u1D65 = 3R/2, C\u209A = 5R/2. Diatomic: C\u1D65 = 5R/2, C\u209A = 7R/2.
(a) C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R for both, so it is not larger. False.
(b) C\u209A + C\u1D65 = 4R (mono) and 6R (di). True.
(c) C\u209A/C\u1D65 = 5/3 (mono) and 7/5 (di), smaller for diatomic. False.
(d) C\u209A\xB7C\u1D65 = 15R\xB2/4 (mono) and 35R\xB2/4 (di). True.
Answer: (b) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"A monoatomic ideal gas, initially at temperature T\u2081, is enclosed in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless piston. The gas is allowed to expand adiabatically to a temperature T\u2082 by releasing the piston suddenly. If L\u2081 and L\u2082 are the lengths of the gas column before and after expansion respectively, then T\u2081/T\u2082 is given by",o:["(L\u2081/L\u2082)^(2/3)","L\u2081/L\u2082","L\u2082/L\u2081","(L\u2082/L\u2081)^(2/3)"],c:3,s:`For an adiabatic change TV\u1D5E\u207B\xB9 = constant.
Monoatomic gas: \u03B3 = 5/3, so \u03B3 \u2212 1 = 2/3.
Volume is proportional to the length L: T\u2081L\u2081^(2/3) = T\u2082L\u2082^(2/3).
T\u2081/T\u2082 = (L\u2082/L\u2081)^(2/3).
Answer: (L\u2082/L\u2081)^(2/3).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"A vessel contains a mixture of one mole of oxygen and two mole of nitrogen at 300 K. The ratio of the average rotational kinetic energy per O\u2082 molecule to that per N\u2082 molecule is",o:["1 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1","depends on the moments of inertia of the two molecules."],c:0,s:`Both O\u2082 and N\u2082 are diatomic and have 2 rotational degrees of freedom.
By equipartition, each degree of freedom has energy \xBDkT.
Average rotational energy per molecule = 2 \xD7 \xBDkT = kT for both, at the same temperature.
So the ratio is 1 : 1, independent of moments of inertia or mole numbers.
Answer: 1 : 1.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"pqk",st:"Molecular speeds (rms, mean, most probable)",q:"Let v\u0304, vrms and vp respectively denote the mean speed, root mean square speed, and most probable speed of the molecules in an ideal monoatomic gas at absolute temperature T. The mass of a molecule is m. Then",o:["no molecule can have a speed greater than \u221A2 vrms","no molecule can have a speed less than vp/\u221A2","vp < v\u0304 < vrms","the average kinetic energy of a molecule is {3;4}mvp\xB2"],c:[2,3],s:`vp = \u221A(2kT/m), v\u0304 = \u221A(8kT/\u03C0m) = 1.128vp, vrms = \u221A(3kT/m) = 1.225vp.
(c) vp < v\u0304 < vrms is true.
(a),(b): the Maxwell distribution has molecules of every speed from 0 to very large, so both are false.
(d) Average KE = \xBDmvrms\xB2 = \xBDm(3/2)vp\xB2 = (3/4)mvp\xB2. True.
Answer: (c) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The average translational energy of O\u2082 (molar mass 32) molecules at a particular temperature is 0.048 eV. The translational kinetic energy of N\u2082 (molar mass 28) molecules in eV at the same temperature is",o:["0.0015","0.003","0.048","0.768"],c:2,s:`Average translational kinetic energy per molecule is (3/2)kT.
It depends only on temperature, not on the mass of the molecule.
So N\u2082 has the same value as O\u2082 at the same temperature.
Answer: 0.048 eV.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The average translational kinetic energy and the root mean square speed of molecules in a sample of oxygen gas at 300 K are 6.21 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 J and 484 m/s respectively. The corresponding values at 600 K are nearly (assuming ideal gas behaviour)",o:["12.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 J, 968 m/s","8.78 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 J, 684 m/s","6.21 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 J, 968 m/s","12.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 J, 684 m/s"],c:3,s:`Average translational KE = (3/2)kT is proportional to T. Doubling T doubles it: 2 \xD7 6.21 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 = 12.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 J.
vrms is proportional to \u221AT, so it becomes \u221A2 times: 484 \xD7 1.414 = 684 m/s.
Answer: 12.42 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 J and 684 m/s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"From the following statements, concerning ideal gas at any given temperature T, select the correct one(s).",o:["The coefficient of volume expansion at constant pressure is the same for all ideal gases","The average translational kinetic energy per molecule of oxygen gas is 3kT, k being Boltzmann constant","The mean free path of molecules increases with increase in the pressure","In a gaseous mixture, the average translational kinetic energy of the molecules of each component is different."],c:0,s:`(a) At constant pressure V = nRT/P, so the coefficient of volume expansion is 1/T for every ideal gas. True.
(b) Average translational KE per molecule is (3/2)kT, not 3kT. False.
(c) Mean free path = kT/(\u221A2 \u03C0d\xB2P) decreases as P increases. False.
(d) Every component has (3/2)kT at the same temperature, so they are equal. False.
Answer: (a) only.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1990",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"When an ideal diatomic gas is heated at constant pressure, the fraction of the heat energy supplied which increases the internal energy of the gas is",o:["{2;5}","{3;5}","{3;7}","{5;7}"],c:3,s:`Heat at constant pressure: Q = nC\u209A\u0394T with C\u209A = 7R/2.
Increase in internal energy: \u0394U = nC\u1D65\u0394T with C\u1D65 = 5R/2.
Fraction = \u0394U/Q = C\u1D65/C\u209A = (5/2)/(7/2) = 5/7.
Answer: 5/7.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"70 calorie of heat is required to raise the temperature of 2 mole of an ideal gas at constant pressure from 30\xB0C to 35\xB0C. The amount of heat required (in calorie) to raise the temperature of the same gas through the same range (30\xB0C to 35\xB0C) at constant volume is",o:["30","50","70","90"],c:1,s:`At constant pressure: 70 = nC\u209A\u0394T = 2 \xD7 C\u209A \xD7 5, so C\u209A = 7 cal/mol K.
C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65 = R \u2248 2 cal/mol K, so C\u1D65 = 5 cal/mol K.
At constant volume: Q = nC\u1D65\u0394T = 2 \xD7 5 \xD7 5 = 50 cal.
Answer: 50 calories.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"\u03B3_A is the specific heat ratio of monoatomic gas A having 3 translational degrees of freedom. \u03B3_B is the specific heat ratio of polyatomic gas B having 3 translational, 3 rotational degrees of freedom and 1 vibrational mode. If \u03B3_A/\u03B3_B = (1 + {1;n}), then the value of n is ______.",n:3,s:`Gas A: f = 3, so \u03B3_A = 1 + 2/3 = 5/3.
Gas B: one vibrational mode counts 2 degrees of freedom, so f = 3 + 3 + 2 = 8.
\u03B3_B = 1 + 2/8 = 5/4.
\u03B3_A/\u03B3_B = (5/3) \xD7 (4/5) = 4/3 = 1 + 1/3.
So n = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"The temperature of 1 mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is increased by 50\xB0C at constant pressure. The total heat added and change in internal energy are E\u2081 and E\u2082, respectively. If E\u2081/E\u2082 = x/9 then the value of x is ______.",n:15,s:`Heat added at constant pressure: E\u2081 = nC\u209A\u0394T = (5/2)R\u0394T (n = 1).
Change in internal energy: E\u2082 = nC\u1D65\u0394T = (3/2)R\u0394T.
E\u2081/E\u2082 = 5/3 = 15/9.
So x = 15.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"A closed container contains a homogeneous mixture of two moles of an ideal monoatomic gas (\u03B3 = 5/3) and one mole of an ideal diatomic gas (\u03B3 = 7/5). Here, \u03B3 is the ratio of the specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume of an ideal gas. The gas mixture does a work of 66 Joule when heated at constant pressure. The change in its internal energy is ______ Joule.",n:121,s:`Total moles n = 3. At constant pressure the work done is W = nR\u0394T = 3R\u0394T = 66 J, so R\u0394T = 22 J.
C\u1D65 of the mixture (total) = 2 \xD7 (3/2)R + 1 \xD7 (5/2)R = 5.5R.
\u0394U = 5.5R\u0394T = 5.5 \xD7 22 = 121 J.
Answer: 121 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"At a certain temperature, the degrees of freedom per molecule for a gas is 8. The gas performs 150 J of work when it expands under constant pressure. The amount of heat absorbed by the gas will be ______ J.",n:750,s:`At constant pressure W = nR\u0394T = 150 J.
\u0394U = (f/2)nR\u0394T = 4 \xD7 150 = 600 J (f = 8).
Heat absorbed Q = \u0394U + W = 600 + 150 = 750 J.
Answer: 750 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Specific heats and \u03B3",q:"One mole of a monoatomic gas is mixed with three moles of a diatomic gas. The molecular specific heat of the mixture at constant volume is {\u03B1\xB2;4} R J/mol K; then the value of \u03B1 will be ______. (Assume that the given diatomic gas has no vibrational mode).",n:3,s:`Monoatomic: C\u1D65 = 3R/2. Diatomic with no vibration: C\u1D65 = 5R/2.
Mixture C\u1D65 = (1 \xD7 3R/2 + 3 \xD7 5R/2)/4 = (9R)/4.
Given C\u1D65 = (\u03B1\xB2/4)R, so \u03B1\xB2 = 9.
\u03B1 = 3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"A monoatomic gas performs a work of {Q;4}, where Q is the heat supplied to it. The molar heat capacity of the gas will be ______ R, during this transformation. Where R is the gas constant.",n:2,s:`First law: Q = \u0394U + W, with W = Q/4, so \u0394U = 3Q/4.
For a monoatomic gas \u0394U = n(3R/2)\u0394T, so n \u0394T = (3Q/4) \xD7 2/(3R) = Q/(2R).
Molar heat capacity C = Q/(n\u0394T) = 2R.
Answer: 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Degrees of freedom and internal energy",q:"The total internal energy of two mole monoatomic ideal gas at temperature T = 300 K will be ______ J. (Given R = 8.31 J/mol K)",n:7479,s:`A monoatomic gas has 3 degrees of freedom.
U = (3/2)nRT.
U = 1.5 \xD7 2 \xD7 8.31 \xD7 300 = 7479 J.
Answer: 7479 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"The average translational kinetic energy of N\u2082 gas molecules at ______ \xB0C becomes equal to the K.E. of an electron accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 0.1 volt. (Given kB = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 J/K) (Fill the nearest integer).",n:500,s:`Kinetic energy of the electron = eV = 0.1 eV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 J.
Set (3/2)kBT = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070.
T = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 / (1.5 \xD7 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3) \u2248 773 K.
In Celsius: 773 \u2212 273 = 500\xB0C.
Answer: 500.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"The temperature of 3.00 mol of an ideal diatomic gas is increased by 40.0\xB0C without changing the pressure of the gas. The molecules in the gas rotate but do not oscillate. If the ratio of change in internal energy of the gas to the amount of workdone by the gas is {x;10}. Then the value of x (round off to the nearest integer) is ______. (Given R = 8.31 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)",n:25,s:`The molecules rotate but do not oscillate, so C\u1D65 = 5R/2.
Change in internal energy \u0394U = nC\u1D65\u0394T = n(5R/2)\u0394T.
Work at constant pressure W = nR\u0394T.
\u0394U/W = 5/2 = 25/10, so x = 25.
Answer: 25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Kinetic energy and temperature",q:"A monoatomic gas of mass 4.0 u is kept in an insulated container. Container is moving with velocity 30 m/s. If container is suddenly stopped then change in temperature of the gas (R = gas constant) is {x;3R}. Value of x is ______.",n:3600,s:`When the container stops, the ordered kinetic energy of the gas becomes random thermal energy.
Per molecule: (3/2)k\u0394T = \xBDmv\xB2, so \u0394T = mv\xB2/(3k).
Multiply top and bottom by Avogadro's number: \u0394T = Mv\xB2/(3R), where M is the molar mass.
Comparing with x/(3R): x = Mv\xB2 = 4 \xD7 30\xB2 = 4 \xD7 900 = 3600 (M = 4 g/mol, v in m/s, as in the official answer).
So x = 3600.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqg",st:"Mixtures and connected vessels",q:"A container is divided into two chambers by a partition. The volume of first chamber is 4.5 litre and second chamber is 5.5 litre. The first chamber contain 3.0 moles of gas at pressure 2.0 atm and second chamber contains 4.0 moles of gas at pressure 3.0 atm. After the partition is removed and the mixture attains equilibrium, then, the common equilibrium pressure existing in the mixture is x \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 atm. Value of x is ______.",n:25.5,s:`Both chambers are at the same temperature and no heat is exchanged, so the temperature stays the same.
Before: P\u2081V\u2081 = n\u2081RT and P\u2082V\u2082 = n\u2082RT. After: P(V\u2081+V\u2082) = (n\u2081+n\u2082)RT.
Hence P(V\u2081+V\u2082) = P\u2081V\u2081 + P\u2082V\u2082.
P \xD7 10 = 2 \xD7 4.5 + 3 \xD7 5.5 = 9 + 16.5 = 25.5, so P = 2.55 atm = 25.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 atm.
So x = 25.5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"1 mole of rigid diatomic gas performs a work of {Q;5} when heat Q is supplied to it. The molar heat capacity of the gas during this transformation is {xR;8}. The value of x is ______. [R = universal gas constant]",n:25,s:`First law: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = Q \u2212 Q/5 = 4Q/5.
For a rigid diatomic gas, \u0394U = nC_V\u0394T = (5/2)R\u0394T (n = 1).
So \u0394T = (4Q/5)/(5R/2) = 8Q/(25R).
Molar heat capacity C = Q/\u0394T = 25R/8, so x = 25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"An engine takes in 5 moles of air at 20\xB0C and 1 atm, and compresses it adiabatically to 1/10th of the original volume. Assuming air to be a diatomic ideal gas made up of rigid molecules, the change in its internal energy during this process comes out to be X kJ. The value of X to the nearest integer is ______.",n:46,s:`Rigid diatomic gas: \u03B3 = 7/5, C_V = 5R/2.
Adiabatic: T\u2082 = T\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(\u03B3\u22121) = 293 \xD7 10^0.4 = 293 \xD7 2.51 \u2248 736 K.
\u0394T = 736 \u2212 293 \u2248 443 K.
\u0394U = nC_V\u0394T = 5 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 8.31 \xD7 443 \u2248 4.6 \xD7 10\u2074 J = 46 kJ.
So X = 46.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"Consider one mole of helium gas enclosed in a container at initial pressure P\u2081 and volume V\u2081. It expands isothermally to volume 4V\u2081. After this, the gas expands adiabatically and its volume becomes 32V\u2081. The work done by the gas during isothermal and adiabatic expansion processes are W_iso and W_adia respectively. If the ratio {W_iso;W_adia} = f ln 2, then f is ______.",n:1.78,s:`Isothermal: W_iso = P\u2081V\u2081 ln(4) = 2P\u2081V\u2081 ln 2.
After it the pressure is P\u2081/4 at volume 4V\u2081, so the gas has P V = P\u2081V\u2081 at the start of the adiabatic step.
Helium: \u03B3 = 5/3. At 32V\u2081: PV = P\u2081V\u2081 \xD7 (4V\u2081/32V\u2081)^(2/3) = P\u2081V\u2081 \xD7 (1/8)^(2/3) = P\u2081V\u2081/4.
W_adia = (P_iV_i \u2212 P_fV_f)/(\u03B3\u22121) = (P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081/4)/(2/3) = (9/8)P\u2081V\u2081.
Ratio = 2 ln 2 /(9/8) = (16/9) ln 2, so f = 16/9 = 1.78.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas undergoes an adiabatic expansion in which its volume becomes eight times its initial volume. If the initial temperature of the gas is 100 K and the universal gas constant R = 8.0 J mol\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB9, the decrease in its internal energy, in joule, is ______.",n:900,s:`Adiabatic: TV^(2/3) = constant for \u03B3 = 5/3.
T\u2082 = 100 \xD7 (1/8)^(2/3) = 100/4 = 25 K.
Decrease in internal energy = nC_V(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) = 1 \xD7 (3/2) \xD7 8.0 \xD7 75 = 900 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:"A diatomic ideal gas is compressed adiabatically to {1;32} of its initial volume. If the initial temperature of the gas is T_i (in Kelvin) and the final temperature is aT_i, the value of a is ______.",n:4,s:`Diatomic gas: \u03B3 = 7/5, so \u03B3 \u2212 1 = 2/5.
T V^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so T_f/T_i = (V_i/V_f)^(2/5) = 32^(2/5).
32 = 2\u2075, so 32^(2/5) = 2\xB2 = 4.
Hence a = 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match the List-I with List-II.
List-I:
A. Triatomic rigid gas
B. Diatomic non-rigid gas
C. Monoatomic gas
D. Diatomic rigid gas
List-II:
I. C_P/C_V = 5/3
II. C_P/C_V = 7/5
III. C_P/C_V = 4/3
IV. C_P/C_V = 9/7
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],c:1,s:`Use \u03B3 = 1 + 2/f (f = total degrees of freedom).
Monoatomic: f = 3, \u03B3 = 5/3 (I). Rigid diatomic: f = 5, \u03B3 = 7/5 (II).
Non-rigid diatomic: f = 7 (one vibration), \u03B3 = 9/7 (IV). Rigid triatomic (non-linear): f = 6, \u03B3 = 4/3 (III).
So A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List I with List II:
List I:
(A) 3 Translational degrees of freedom
(B) 3 Translational, 2 rotational degrees of freedoms
(C) 3 Translational, 2 rotational and 1 vibrational degrees of freedom
(D) 3 Translational, 3 rotational and more than one vibrational degrees of freedom
List II:
(I) Monoatomic gases
(II) Polyatomic gases
(III) Rigid diatomic gases
(IV) Nonrigid diatomic gases
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],c:3,s:`Count the ways each type of molecule can move.
A single atom only translates: A \u2192 Monoatomic (I).
A rigid diatomic molecule translates and rotates about two axes: B \u2192 Rigid diatomic (III).
If it also vibrates (one mode) it is non-rigid diatomic: C \u2192 (IV).
A polyatomic molecule has 3 rotations and many vibrations: D \u2192 Polyatomic (II).
So A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`List I describes thermodynamic processes in four different systems. List II gives the magnitudes (either exactly or as a close approximation) of possible changes in the internal energy of the system due to the process.
List-I:
(I) 10\u207B\xB3 kg of water at 100\xB0C is converted to steam at the same temperature, at a pressure of 10\u2075 Pa. The volume of the system changes from 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3 to 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 in the process. Latent heat of water = 2250 kJ/kg.
(II) 0.2 moles of a rigid diatomic ideal gas with volume V at temperature 500 K undergoes an isobaric expansion to volume 3V. Assume R = 8.0 J mol\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB9.
(III) One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas is compressed adiabatically from volume V = {1;3} m\xB3 and pressure 2 kPa to volume V/8.
(IV) Three moles of a diatomic ideal gas whose molecules can vibrate, is given 9 kJ of heat and undergoes isobaric expansion.
List-II:
(P) 2 kJ
(Q) 7 kJ
(R) 4 kJ
(S) 5 kJ
(T) 3 kJ
Which one of the following options is correct?`,o:["I \u2192 T, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 S, IV \u2192 Q","I \u2192 S, II \u2192 P, III \u2192 T, IV \u2192 P","I \u2192 P, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 T, IV \u2192 Q","I \u2192 Q, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 S, IV \u2192 T"],c:2,s:`(I) Q = 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 2250 kJ = 2250 J. Work done by system = P\u0394V = 10\u2075 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u2248 100 J. \u0394U = 2250 \u2212 100 \u2248 2.15 kJ \u2248 2 kJ (P).
(II) V \u2192 3V at constant P, so T \u2192 1500 K. \u0394U = nC_V\u0394T = 0.2 \xD7 (5/2) \xD7 8 \xD7 1000 = 4000 J = 4 kJ (R).
(III) P\u2081V\u2081 = 2000 \xD7 (1/3) = 667 J. Adiabatic: T\u2082 = T\u2081 \xD7 8^(2/3) = 4T\u2081. \u0394U = (3/2)(P\u2081V\u2081)(4 \u2212 1) = 3000 J = 3 kJ (T).
(IV) A vibrating diatomic gas has f = 7, C_P = 9R/2, C_V = 7R/2. \u0394U = Q \xD7 C_V/C_P = 9 \xD7 7/9 = 7 kJ (Q).
So I\u2192P, II\u2192R, III\u2192T, IV\u2192Q.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match the C_P/C_V ratio for ideal gases with different type of molecules.
Molecule type:
(A) Monatomic
(B) Diatomic rigid molecules
(C) Diatomic non-rigid molecules
(D) Triatomic rigid molecules
C_P/C_V:
(I) 7/5
(II) 9/7
(III) 4/3
(IV) 5/3`,o:["(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"],c:3,s:`Use \u03B3 = 1 + 2/f.
Monatomic: f = 3, \u03B3 = 5/3 (IV). Rigid diatomic: f = 5, \u03B3 = 7/5 (I).
Non-rigid diatomic: f = 7, \u03B3 = 9/7 (II). Rigid triatomic: f = 6, \u03B3 = 4/3 (III).
So A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Answer Q. 196 and Q. 197 by appropriately matching the lists based on the information given in the paragraph.
In a thermodynamic process on an ideal monatomic gas, the infinitesimal heat absorbed by the gas is given by T\u0394X, where T is temperature of the system and \u0394X is the infinitesimal change in a thermodynamic quantity X of the system. For a mole of monatomic ideal gas X = {3;2}R ln({T;T_A}) + R ln({V;V_A}). Here, R is gas constant, V is volume of gas, T_A and V_A are constants.
The List-I gives some quantities involved in a process and List-II gives some possible values of these quantities.
For the process on one mole of monatomic ideal gas shown in the TV-diagram, with P\u2080V\u2080 = {1;3}RT\u2080, the correct match is,
List-I:
(I) Work done by the system in process 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3
(II) Change in internal energy in process 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3
(III) Heat absorbed by the system in process 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3
(IV) Heat absorbed by the system in process 1 \u2192 2
List-II:
(P) {1;3}RT\u2080 ln 2
(Q) {1;3}RT\u2080
(R) RT\u2080
(S) {4;3}RT\u2080
(T) {1;3}RT\u2080(3 + ln 2)
(U) {5;6}RT\u2080
`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"TV diagram: point 1 at volume V0 and temperature T0/3, point 2 at volume 2V0 and the same temperature, point 3 at volume 2V0 and temperature T0; the path goes 1 to 2 horizontally and then 2 to 3 vertically",d:[["l",60,175,60,18,"a"],["l",60,175,310,175,"a"],["t",50,26,"T","e"],["t",312,179,"V"],["l",60,40,230,40,"d"],["l",60,127,130,127,"d"],["l",130,127,130,175,"d"],["l",230,127,230,175,"d"],["t",54,44,"T\u2080","e"],["t",54,131,"T\u2080/3","e"],["t",130,191,"V\u2080","m"],["t",230,191,"2V\u2080","m"],["l",130,127,224,127,"a"],["l",230,127,230,46,"a"],["c",130,127,2.5,"F"],["c",230,127,2.5,"F"],["c",230,40,2.5,"F"],["t",128,118,"1","e"],["t",237,140,"2"],["t",237,38,"3"]]},o:["I \u2192 P, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 T, IV \u2192 S","I \u2192 P, II \u2192 T, III \u2192 Q, IV \u2192 T","I \u2192 S, II \u2192 T, III \u2192 Q, IV \u2192 U","I \u2192 P, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 T, IV \u2192 P"],c:3,s:`Process 1\u21922 is at constant T (isothermal, T\u2080/3), expansion from V\u2080 to 2V\u2080. Process 2\u21923 is at constant volume 2V\u2080, T rises from T\u2080/3 to T\u2080.
(I) Work: only 1\u21922 does work. W = RT ln 2 = (1/3)RT\u2080 ln 2 \u2192 (P).
(II) \u0394U = C_V\u0394T = (3/2)R(T\u2080 \u2212 T\u2080/3) = RT\u2080 \u2192 (R).
(III) Q = W + \u0394U = (1/3)RT\u2080 ln 2 + RT\u2080 = (1/3)RT\u2080(3 + ln 2) \u2192 (T).
(IV) In 1\u21922, \u0394U = 0, so Q = W = (1/3)RT\u2080 ln 2 \u2192 (P).
So I\u2192P, II\u2192R, III\u2192T, IV\u2192P.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Answer Q. 196 and Q. 197 by appropriately matching the lists based on the information given in the paragraph.
In a thermodynamic process on an ideal monatomic gas, the infinitesimal heat absorbed by the gas is given by T\u0394X, where T is temperature of the system and \u0394X is the infinitesimal change in a thermodynamic quantity X of the system. For a mole of monatomic ideal gas X = {3;2}R ln({T;T_A}) + R ln({V;V_A}). Here, R is gas constant, V is volume of gas, T_A and V_A are constants.
The List-I gives some quantities involved in a process and List-II gives some possible values of these quantities.
If the process carried out on one mole of monoatomic ideal gas is as shown in the PV-diagram with P\u2080V\u2080 = {1;3}RT\u2080, the correct match is,
List-I:
(I) Work done by the system in process 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3
(II) Change in internal energy in process 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3
(III) Heat absorbed by the system in process 1 \u2192 2 \u2192 3
(IV) Heat absorbed by the system in process 1 \u2192 2
List-II:
(P) {1;3}RT\u2080 ln 2
(Q) {1;3}RT\u2080
(R) RT\u2080
(S) {4;3}RT\u2080
(T) {1;3}RT\u2080(3 + ln 2)
(U) {5;6}RT\u2080
`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"PV diagram: point 1 at volume V0 and pressure P0, point 2 at volume 2V0 and pressure P0, point 3 at volume 2V0 and pressure 3P0/2; the path goes 1 to 2 horizontally and then 2 to 3 vertically",d:[["l",60,175,60,18,"a"],["l",60,175,310,175,"a"],["t",50,26,"P","e"],["t",312,179,"V"],["l",60,85,230,85,"d"],["l",60,115,130,115,"d"],["l",130,115,130,175,"d"],["l",230,115,230,175,"d"],["t",54,89,"3P\u2080/2","e"],["t",54,119,"P\u2080","e"],["t",130,191,"V\u2080","m"],["t",230,191,"2V\u2080","m"],["l",130,115,224,115,"a"],["l",230,115,230,91,"a"],["c",130,115,2.5,"F"],["c",230,115,2.5,"F"],["c",230,85,2.5,"F"],["t",128,106,"1","e"],["t",237,128,"2"],["t",237,82,"3"]]},o:["I \u2192 S, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 Q, IV \u2192 T","I \u2192 Q, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 P, IV \u2192 U","I \u2192 Q, II \u2192 S, III \u2192 R, IV \u2192 U","I \u2192 Q, II \u2192 R, III \u2192 S, IV \u2192 U"],c:3,s:`Process 1\u21922 is isobaric at P\u2080 (V\u2080 to 2V\u2080). Process 2\u21923 is isochoric at 2V\u2080 (P\u2080 to 3P\u2080/2).
(I) Work = P\u2080(2V\u2080 \u2212 V\u2080) = P\u2080V\u2080 = (1/3)RT\u2080 \u2192 (Q).
(II) \u0394U = (3/2)(P\u2083V\u2083 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081) = (3/2)(3P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 P\u2080V\u2080) = 3P\u2080V\u2080 = RT\u2080 \u2192 (R).
(III) Q = W + \u0394U = (1/3)RT\u2080 + RT\u2080 = (4/3)RT\u2080 \u2192 (S).
(IV) Isobaric: Q = nC_P\u0394T = (5/2)P\u2080(2V\u2080 \u2212 V\u2080) = (5/2)P\u2080V\u2080 = (5/6)RT\u2080 \u2192 (U).
So I\u2192Q, II\u2192R, III\u2192S, IV\u2192U.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas is taken along two cyclic processes E \u2192 F \u2192 G \u2192 E and E \u2192 F \u2192 H \u2192 E as shown in the PV diagram. The processes involved are purely isochoric, isobaric, isothermal or adiabatic. Match the paths in List I with the magnitudes of the work done in List II and select the correct answer using the codes given below the lists.
List I:
(P) G \u2192 E
(Q) G \u2192 H
(R) F \u2192 H
(S) F \u2192 G
List II:
(1) 160 P\u2080V\u2080 ln2
(2) 36 P\u2080V\u2080
(3) 24 P\u2080V\u2080
(4) 31 P\u2080V\u2080
Codes (P, Q, R, S):`,fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"PV diagram: E at volume V0 and pressure P0, F directly above E at 32P0; from F an isothermal curve falls to G on the P0 line far to the right and a steeper adiabatic curve falls to H on the P0 line nearer to E; the P0 line is traversed from G back to E",d:[["l",60,175,60,12,"a"],["l",60,175,320,175,"a"],["t",50,20,"P","e"],["t",322,179,"V"],["l",60,150,110,150,"d"],["l",60,30,110,30,"d"],["l",110,150,110,175,"d"],["t",54,34,"32P\u2080","e"],["t",54,154,"P\u2080","e"],["t",110,191,"V\u2080","m"],["l",110,150,110,36,"a"],["p","M110,30 Q135,150 300,150"],["p","M110,30 Q115,140 190,150"],["l",300,150,114,150,"a"],["t",116,26,"F"],["t",98,163,"E"],["t",304,154,"G"],["t",190,166,"H","m"]]},o:["4, 3, 2, 1","4, 3, 1, 2","3, 1, 2, 4","1, 3, 2, 4"],c:0,s:`E\u2192F is isochoric. G and H lie on the P\u2080 line, so G\u2192E and G\u2192H are isobaric. F\u2192G is isothermal, F\u2192H is adiabatic (\u03B3 = 5/3).
F\u2192G: P V stays 32P\u2080V\u2080, so V_G = 32V\u2080. W = 32P\u2080V\u2080 ln 32 = 160 P\u2080V\u2080 ln 2 \u2192 S = (1).
F\u2192H: 32P\u2080V\u2080^(5/3) = P\u2080V_H^(5/3) gives V_H = 8V\u2080. W = (32P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 P\u2080\xB78V\u2080)/(2/3) = 36 P\u2080V\u2080 \u2192 R = (2).
G\u2192E: W = P\u2080(V\u2080 \u2212 32V\u2080) = \u221231P\u2080V\u2080, magnitude 31 \u2192 P = (4).
G\u2192H: W = P\u2080(8V\u2080 \u2212 32V\u2080) = \u221224P\u2080V\u2080, magnitude 24 \u2192 Q = (3).
So P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`A container has a movable (frictionless) piston on top. The container and the piston are made of perfectly insulating material, allowing no heat transfer between outside and inside the container. The container is divided into two compartments by a rigid partition made of a thermally conducting material that allows slow transfer of heat. The lower compartment is filled with 2 moles of an ideal monoatomic gas at 700 K and the upper compartment is filled with 2 moles of an ideal diatomic gas at 400 K. The heat capacities per mole of an ideal monoatomic gas are C_V = {3;2}R, C_P = {5;2}R and those for an ideal diatomic gas are C_V = {5;2}R, C_P = {7;2}R.
Now consider the partition to be rigidly fixed so that it does not move. When equilibrium is achieved, the final temperature of the gases will be`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A closed insulated container with a movable piston on top and a horizontal conducting partition: upper compartment diatomic gas 2 mol at 400 K, lower compartment monoatomic gas 2 mol at 700 K",d:[["r",110,40,120,110],["r",110,28,120,10,"F"],["l",170,6,170,26,"a"],["l",110,95,230,95,"B"],["t",240,36,"Piston"],["t",240,70,"Diatomic gas"],["t",240,84,"2 mol, 400 K","s"],["t",240,122,"Monoatomic gas"],["t",240,136,"2 mol, 700 K","s"],["t",104,99,"Partition","e"]]},o:["550 K","525 K","513 K","490 K"],c:3,s:`The partition is fixed, so the lower (monoatomic) gas is heated at constant volume. The upper (diatomic) gas is under the free piston, so it is heated at constant pressure.
Heat lost by lower gas = heat gained by upper gas:
2 \xD7 (3R/2) \xD7 (700 \u2212 T) = 2 \xD7 (7R/2) \xD7 (T \u2212 400).
3(700 \u2212 T) = 7(T \u2212 400), so 2100 \u2212 3T = 7T \u2212 2800.
10T = 4900, so T = 490 K.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"pqd",st:"Work, heat and processes of a gas",q:`A container has a movable (frictionless) piston on top. The container and the piston are made of perfectly insulating material, allowing no heat transfer between outside and inside the container. The container is divided into two compartments by a rigid partition made of a thermally conducting material that allows slow transfer of heat. The lower compartment is filled with 2 moles of an ideal monoatomic gas at 700 K and the upper compartment is filled with 2 moles of an ideal diatomic gas at 400 K. The heat capacities per mole of an ideal monoatomic gas are C_V = {3;2}R, C_P = {5;2}R and those for an ideal diatomic gas are C_V = {5;2}R, C_P = {7;2}R.
Now consider the partition to be free to move without friction so that the pressure of gases in both compartments is the same. Then total work done by the gases till the time they achieve equilibrium will be`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"A closed insulated container with a movable piston on top and a horizontal conducting partition: upper compartment diatomic gas 2 mol at 400 K, lower compartment monoatomic gas 2 mol at 700 K",d:[["r",110,40,120,110],["r",110,28,120,10,"F"],["l",170,6,170,26,"a"],["l",110,95,230,95,"B"],["t",240,36,"Piston"],["t",240,70,"Diatomic gas"],["t",240,84,"2 mol, 400 K","s"],["t",240,122,"Monoatomic gas"],["t",240,136,"2 mol, 700 K","s"],["t",104,99,"Partition","e"]]},o:["250R","200R","100R","\u2212100R"],c:3,s:`Both gases stay at the same constant pressure (set by the free piston), so both are heated or cooled at constant pressure.
Heat balance: 2 \xD7 (5R/2)(700 \u2212 T) = 2 \xD7 (7R/2)(T \u2212 400).
5(700 \u2212 T) = 7(T \u2212 400), so 3500 \u2212 5T = 7T \u2212 2800, T = 525 K.
Work at constant pressure: W = nR\u0394T.
Lower gas: 2R(525 \u2212 700) = \u2212350R. Upper gas: 2R(525 \u2212 400) = +250R.
Total work = \u2212350R + 250R = \u2212100R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements.
Statement I : The temperature of a gas is \u221273\xB0C. When the gas is heated to 527\xB0C, the root mean square speed of the molecules is doubled.
Statement II : The product of pressure and volume of an ideal gas will be equal to translational kinetic energy of the molecules.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is false but statement II is true.","Both statement I and statement II are true.","Both statement I and statement II are false.","Statement I is true but statement II is false."],c:3,s:`Statement I: T\u2081 = \u221273 + 273 = 200 K and T\u2082 = 527 + 273 = 800 K. v_rms \u221D \u221AT, so v\u2082/v\u2081 = \u221A(800/200) = 2. True.
Statement II: The total translational KE is (3/2)nRT = (3/2)PV. So PV = (2/3) \xD7 KE, not equal to KE. False.
Statement I is true but Statement II is false.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements.
Assertion (A) : In a diatomic molecule, the rotational energy at a given temperature obeys Maxwell's distribution.
Reason (R) : In a diatomic molecule, the rotational energy at a given temperature equals the translational kinetic energy for each molecule.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],c:2,s:`The energies of the molecules, including rotational energy, are spread out over the molecules according to the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution at a given temperature, so (A) is taken as true.
For a diatomic molecule the average translational KE is (3/2)kT (3 degrees of freedom).
The average rotational KE is 2 \xD7 \xBDkT = kT (2 degrees of freedom).
These are not equal, so (R) is false.
(A) is true but (R) is false.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2007",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Assertion (A) : The total translational kinetic energy of all the molecules of a given mass of an ideal gas is 1.5 times the product of its pressure and its volume.
Reason (R) : The molecules of a gas collide with each other and the velocities of the molecules change due to the collision.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],c:1,s:`Total translational KE = (3/2)nRT = (3/2)PV, so the Assertion is true.
The Reason is also a true fact: collisions do change molecular velocities.
But collisions are not why KE = (3/2)PV; that follows from the relation between pressure and the mean KE of the molecules.
So both are true but R is not the correct explanation.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A diatomic gas is enclosed in a vessel fitted with a massless movable piston. Area of cross section of vessel is 1 m\xB2. Initial height of the piston is 1 m. The initial temperature of the gas is 300 K. The temperature of the gas is increased to 400 K, keeping pressure constant. Calculate the new height of the piston. The piston is brought to its initial position with no heat exchange. Calculate the final temperature of the gas. You can leave answer in fraction.",a:"New height = 4/3 m; final temperature = 400 \xD7 (4/3)^(2/5) K (about 449 K)",s:`Constant pressure: V/T is constant. Volume = area \xD7 height, so h\u2082 = h\u2081 \xD7 T\u2082/T\u2081 = 1 \xD7 400/300 = 4/3 m.
Now the piston is pushed back to 1 m without heat exchange, so the compression is adiabatic from V = 4/3 m\xB3 to V = 1 m\xB3.
Diatomic gas: \u03B3 = 7/5, so \u03B3 \u2212 1 = 2/5.
TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant gives T' = 400 \xD7 (4/3)^(2/5) K \u2248 449 K.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A monoatomic ideal gas of two moles is taken through a cyclic process starting from A as shown in the figure (a V\u2013T graph). The volume ratios are {V_B;V_A} = 2 and {V_D;V_A} = 4. If the temperature T_A at A is 27\xB0C, calculate
(a) the temperature of the gas at point B,
(b) heat absorbed or released by the gas in each process,
(c) the total work done by the gas during the complete cycle.
Express your answer in terms of the gas constant R.`,fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"VT diagram of a cycle ABCDA: straight line through the origin from A to B, vertical line up from B to C, horizontal line left from C to D at the same volume, vertical line down from D to A",d:[["l",50,215,50,10,"a"],["l",50,215,320,215,"a"],["t",44,18,"V","e"],["t",322,219,"T"],["t",44,230,"0","e"],["l",50,215,150,165],["l",150,165,190,145,"a"],["l",190,145,190,79,"a"],["l",190,75,124,75,"a"],["l",120,75,120,176,"a"],["l",50,180,120,180,"d"],["l",50,145,190,145,"d"],["l",50,75,120,75,"d"],["l",120,180,120,215,"d"],["l",190,145,190,215,"d"],["t",44,184,"V_A","e"],["t",44,149,"V_B","e"],["t",44,79,"V_D","e"],["t",120,231,"T_A","m"],["t",190,231,"T_B","m"],["t",128,176,"A"],["t",196,148,"B"],["t",196,72,"C"],["t",106,70,"D"]]},a:"(a) 600 K   (b) Q_AB = +1500R, Q_BC = +831.8R, Q_CD = \u2212900R, Q_DA = \u2212831.8R   (c) 600R",s:`Read the graph: A\u2192B is a straight line through the origin (V \u221D T, isobaric). B\u2192C is vertical (T constant, isothermal expansion). C\u2192D is horizontal (V constant, isochoric cooling). D\u2192A is vertical (T constant, isothermal compression). T_A = 300 K, n = 2.
(a) V/T constant on AB: T_B = T_A \xD7 V_B/V_A = 600 K.
(b) A\u2192B: Q = nC_P\u0394T = 2 \xD7 (5R/2) \xD7 300 = +1500R.
B\u2192C: Q = nRT_B ln(V_D/V_B) = 2R \xD7 600 \xD7 ln 2 = +831.8R.
C\u2192D: Q = nC_V\u0394T = 2 \xD7 (3R/2) \xD7 (\u2212300) = \u2212900R.
D\u2192A: Q = nRT_A ln(V_A/V_D) = 2R \xD7 300 \xD7 ln(1/4) = \u2212831.8R.
(c) Over a full cycle \u0394U = 0, so W = net Q = 1500R + 831.8R \u2212 900R \u2212 831.8R = 600R.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two moles of an ideal monoatomic gas, initially at pressure P\u2081 and volume V\u2081, undergo an adiabatic compression until its volume is V\u2082. Then the gas is given heat Q at constant volume V\u2082.
(a) Sketch the complete process on a P\u2013V diagram.
(b) Find the total work done by the gas, the total change in its internal energy and the final temperature of the gas. [Give your answer in terms of P\u2081, V\u2081, V\u2082, Q and R.]`,a:"W = (3P\u2081V\u2081/2)[1 \u2212 (V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3)];  \u0394U = Q + (3P\u2081V\u2081/2)[(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3) \u2212 1];  T_f = (P\u2081V\u2081/2R)(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3) + Q/(3R)",s:`(a) On the P\u2013V diagram: start at (V\u2081, P\u2081), go along a steep adiabatic curve to the left to (V\u2082, P\u2082) with P\u2082 = P\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(5/3). Then go straight up (constant volume V\u2082) to a higher pressure.
(b) Work is done only in the adiabatic step. P\u2082V\u2082 = P\u2081V\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3) and \u03B3 = 5/3.
W = (P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082V\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = (3P\u2081V\u2081/2)[1 \u2212 (V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3)] (negative, since the gas is compressed).
First law for the whole process: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = Q + (3P\u2081V\u2081/2)[(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3) \u2212 1].
Final temperature: T\u2081 = P\u2081V\u2081/2R. After compression T\u2082 = T\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3). Heating at constant volume: T_f = T\u2082 + Q/(nC_V) = T\u2082 + Q/(3R).
So T_f = (P\u2081V\u2081/2R)(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(2/3) + Q/(3R).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two moles of an ideal monoatomic gas are taken through a cycle ABCA as shown in the P\u2013T diagram. During the process AB, pressure and temperature of the gas vary such that PT = constant. If T\u2081 = 300 K, calculate
(a) the work done on the gas in the process AB and
(b) the heat absorbed or released by the gas in each of the processes.
(Give answers in terms of R.)`,fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"PT diagram of cycle ABCA: A at temperature 2T1 and pressure P1, B at temperature T1 and pressure 2P1 joined to A by a curve with PT constant, C at 2T1 and 2P1; B to C horizontal, C to A vertical downward",d:[["l",50,175,50,15,"a"],["l",50,175,320,175,"a"],["t",44,22,"P","e"],["t",322,179,"T"],["l",50,125,230,125,"d"],["l",50,75,130,75,"d"],["l",130,75,130,175,"d"],["l",230,125,230,175,"d"],["t",44,129,"P\u2081","e"],["t",44,79,"2P\u2081","e"],["t",130,191,"T\u2081","m"],["t",230,191,"2T\u2081","m"],["p","M230,125 L217.5,121.7 L205,118 L192.5,113.5 L180,108.3 L167.5,102.3 L155,95 L142.5,86.1 L130,75","a"],["l",130,75,224,75,"a"],["l",230,75,230,119,"a"],["t",236,140,"A"],["t",124,68,"B","e"],["t",236,70,"C"]]},a:"(a) +1200R (work done on the gas)   (b) Q_AB = \u22122100R, Q_BC = +1500R, Q_CA = +831.8R",s:`From the graph: A = (2T\u2081, P\u2081), B = (T\u2081, 2P\u2081), C = (2T\u2081, 2P\u2081). AB has PT = constant, BC is isobaric (P = 2P\u2081), CA is isothermal (T = 2T\u2081 = 600 K).
(a) PV = nRT and PT = c give V = nRT\xB2/c. So P dV = (c/T) \xD7 (2nRT/c) dT = 2nR dT.
Work by gas W_AB = 2nR(T_B \u2212 T_A) = 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 R \xD7 (300 \u2212 600) = \u22121200R, so work done ON the gas = +1200R.
(b) AB: \u0394U = nC_V\u0394T = 2 \xD7 (3R/2) \xD7 (\u2212300) = \u2212900R. Q_AB = \u0394U + W = \u2212900R \u2212 1200R = \u22122100R (released).
BC: isobaric, Q = nC_P\u0394T = 2 \xD7 (5R/2) \xD7 300 = +1500R (absorbed).
CA: isothermal, Q = nRT ln(P_C/P_A) = 2R \xD7 600 \xD7 ln 2 = +831.8R (absorbed).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A sample of 2 kg of monoatomic helium (assumed ideal) is taken through the process ABC and another sample of 2 kg of the same gas is taken through the process ADC (see figure; pressure in units of 10\u2074 N/m\xB2). Given molecular mass of helium = 4.
(i) What is the temperature of helium in each of the states A, B, C and D?
(ii) Is there any way of telling afterwards which sample of helium went through the process ABC and which went through the process ADC? Write yes or no.
(iii) How much is the heat involved in each of the processes ABC and ADC?`,fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"PV diagram of a rectangle: A at volume 10 and pressure 5, B above A at pressure 10, C at volume 20 and pressure 10, D at volume 20 and pressure 5; pressure in units of 10^4 N/m2, volume in m3",d:[["l",60,175,60,28,"a"],["l",60,175,300,175,"a"],["t",66,20,"P (10\u2074 N/m\xB2)"],["t",250,191,"V (m\xB3)"],["r",130,70,100,50],["l",60,70,130,70,"d"],["l",60,120,130,120,"d"],["l",130,120,130,175,"d"],["l",230,120,230,175,"d"],["t",54,74,"10","e"],["t",54,124,"5","e"],["t",130,191,"10","m"],["t",230,191,"20","m"],["t",126,134,"A","e"],["t",126,66,"B","e"],["t",236,66,"C"],["t",236,134,"D"]]},a:"(i) T_A = 120.3 K, T_B = T_D = 240.6 K, T_C = 481.2 K   (ii) No   (iii) ABC: 3.25 \xD7 10\u2076 J, ADC: 2.75 \xD7 10\u2076 J",s:`n = 2000/4 = 500 mol. Read the graph: A (10 m\xB3, 5\xD710\u2074), B (10 m\xB3, 10\xD710\u2074), C (20 m\xB3, 10\xD710\u2074), D (20 m\xB3, 5\xD710\u2074). Use T = PV/nR with nR = 500 \xD7 8.31 = 4155.
(i) T_A = 5\xD710\u2075/4155 = 120.3 K; T_B = 10\xD710\u2075/4155 = 240.6 K; T_C = 20\xD710\u2075/4155 = 481.2 K; T_D = 10\xD710\u2075/4155 = 240.6 K.
(ii) No. Both samples end in the same state C, and the final state does not record the path.
(iii) \u0394U (A\u2192C) = nC_V\u0394T = (3/2)(P_CV_C \u2212 P_AV_A) = 1.5 \xD7 (20 \u2212 5) \xD7 10\u2075 = 2.25 \xD7 10\u2076 J for both.
ABC: work = area under BC = 10\u2075 \xD7 10 = 1.0 \xD7 10\u2076 J, so Q = 3.25 \xD7 10\u2076 J.
ADC: work = area under AD = 5\xD710\u2074 \xD7 10 = 0.5 \xD7 10\u2076 J, so Q = 2.75 \xD7 10\u2076 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1990",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`An ideal gas having initial pressure P, volume V and temperature T is allowed to expand adiabatically until its volume becomes 5.66 V while its temperature falls to T/2.
(i) How many degrees of freedom do the gas molecules have?
(ii) Obtain the work done by the gas during the expansion as a function of the initial pressure P and volume V.`,a:"(i) f = 5 (\u03B3 = 1.4)   (ii) W = 5PV/4 = 1.25 PV",s:`(i) Adiabatic: TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so T \xD7 V^(\u03B3\u22121) = (T/2)(5.66V)^(\u03B3\u22121).
So 5.66^(\u03B3\u22121) = 2. Since 5.66 \u2248 2^2.5, (\u03B3\u22121) \xD7 2.5 = 1, so \u03B3 \u2212 1 = 0.4 and \u03B3 = 1.4.
\u03B3 = 1 + 2/f gives f = 2/0.4 = 5 (a diatomic gas).
(ii) W = (P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082V\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1). P\u2081V\u2081 = PV and P\u2082V\u2082 = nR(T/2) = PV/2.
W = (PV \u2212 PV/2)/0.4 = 1.25 PV = 5PV/4.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"An ideal monoatomic gas is confined in a cylinder by a spring-loaded piston of cross-section 8.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2. Initially the gas is at 300 K and occupies a volume of 2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 and the spring is in its relaxed (unstretched, uncompressed) state (see figure). The gas is heated by a small electric heater until the piston moves out slowly by 0.1 m. Calculate the final temperature of the gas and the heat supplied (in joule) by the heater. The force constant of the spring is 8000 N/m, atmospheric pressure is 1.0 \xD7 10\u2075 Nm\u207B\xB2. The cylinder and the piston are thermally insulated. The piston is massless and there is no friction between the piston and the cylinder. Neglect heat loss through lead wires of the heater. The heat capacity of the heater coil is negligible. Assume the spring to be massless.",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Horizontal cylinder with a heater in the gas on the left, a movable piston, and a spring on the right fixed to a rigid support in the open atmosphere",d:[["p","M80,40 H262 M80,40 V120 M80,120 H262"],["r",166,42,8,76,"F"],["l",62,64,100,64],["l",62,96,100,96],["p","M100,64 L108,70 L96,78 L108,86 L96,92 L100,96"],["p","M174,80 H190 L194,70 L202,90 L210,70 L218,90 L226,70 L230,80 H268"],["l",270,60,270,100,"B"],["t",60,54,"Heater"],["t",112,84,"Gas"],["t",178,34,"Open atmosphere"],["t",228,138,"Rigid support"],["t",196,64,"Spring","s"]]},a:"T = 800 K, heat supplied = 720 J",s:`Initially the spring is relaxed, so P\u2081 = P_atm = 1.0 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
After moving x = 0.1 m: V\u2082 = 2.4\xD710\u207B\xB3 + 8\xD710\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.1 = 3.2\xD710\u207B\xB3 m\xB3. P\u2082 = P_atm + kx/A = 10\u2075 + 800/(8\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
T\u2082 = T\u2081 \xD7 (P\u2082V\u2082)/(P\u2081V\u2081) = 300 \xD7 (2 \xD7 3.2)/(1 \xD7 2.4) = 800 K.
\u0394U = (3/2)(P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081) = 1.5 \xD7 (640 \u2212 240) = 600 J.
Work done by gas = P_atm \u0394V + \xBDkx\xB2 = 10\u2075 \xD7 8\xD710\u207B\u2074 + \xBD \xD7 8000 \xD7 0.01 = 80 + 40 = 120 J.
Q = \u0394U + W = 600 + 120 = 720 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"An ideal gas has a specific heat at constant pressure C_P = {5R;2}. The gas is kept in a closed vessel of volume 0.0083 m\xB3, at a temperature of 300 K and a pressure of 1.6 \xD7 10\u2076 N/m\xB2. An amount of 2.49 \xD7 10\u2074 joule of heat energy is supplied to the gas. Calculate the final temperature and pressure of the gas.",a:"T = 675 K, P = 3.6 \xD7 10\u2076 N m\u207B\xB2",s:`C_V = C_P \u2212 R = 3R/2. Number of moles n = PV/RT = (1.6\xD710\u2076 \xD7 0.0083)/(8.3 \xD7 300) = 16/3 \u2248 5.33 mol.
Volume is constant, so Q = nC_V\u0394T.
\u0394T = 2.49\xD710\u2074 /[(16/3) \xD7 (3/2) \xD7 8.3] = 24900/66.4 = 375 K.
T_f = 300 + 375 = 675 K.
Constant volume: P_f = P \xD7 T_f/T_i = 1.6\xD710\u2076 \xD7 675/300 = 3.6 \xD7 10\u2076 N/m\xB2.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"pqk",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`One gram mole of oxygen at 27\xB0C and one atmosphere pressure is enclosed in a vessel.
(i) Assuming the molecules to be moving with v_rms, find the number of collisions per second which the molecules make with one square metre area of the vessel wall.
(ii) The vessel is next thermally insulated and moved with a constant speed v\u2080. It is then suddenly stopped. The process results in a rise of the temperature of the gas by 1\xB0C. Calculate the speed v\u2080.`,a:"(i) 1.97 \xD7 10\xB2\u2077 collisions per second per m\xB2   (ii) v\u2080 \u2248 35.6 m/s",s:`(i) Molar mass of O\u2082 = 32 g, so m = 0.032/6.02\xD710\xB2\xB3 = 5.3\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2076 kg. v_rms = \u221A(3RT/M) = \u221A(3 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 300/0.032) = 483 m/s.
Each collision reverses the normal velocity, so the momentum change is 2mu. If Z is the number of collisions per second per m\xB2, pressure = 2muZ.
Z = P/(2mu) = 1.01\xD710\u2075/(2 \xD7 5.3\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2076 \xD7 483) \u2248 1.97 \xD7 10\xB2\u2077 per second per m\xB2.
(ii) The kinetic energy of the moving gas becomes internal energy: \xBDMv\u2080\xB2 = C_V \xD7 1 K (for 1 mole).
For oxygen take \u03B3 = 1.41, so C_V = R/(\u03B3\u22121) = R/0.41.
v\u2080\xB2 = 2R/(0.41 \xD7 M) = 2 \xD7 8.3/(0.41 \xD7 0.032) \u2248 1265, so v\u2080 \u2248 35.6 m/s.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"At a given temperature, the specific heat of a gas at constant pressure is always greater than its specific heat at constant volume.",o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`At constant volume all the heat only raises the temperature.
At constant pressure the gas also expands and does work, so more heat is needed for the same temperature rise.
Mathematically C_P \u2212 C_V = R > 0 per mole. So the statement is True.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"pqd",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas is mixed with one mole of a diatomic ideal gas. The molar specific heat of the mixture at constant volume is ______.",a:"2R",s:`Heat needed to raise the temperature of the mixture by 1 K at constant volume = n\u2081C_V1 + n\u2082C_V2 = (3R/2) + (5R/2) = 4R for 2 moles.
Molar specific heat of the mixture = 4R/2 = 2R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"The mean free path and the average speed of oxygen molecules at 300 K and 1 atm are 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m and 600 m/s, respectively. Find the frequency of its collisions.",o:["2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076 /s","5 \xD7 10\u2078 /s","9 \xD7 10\u2075 /s","2 \xD7 10\u2079 /s"],c:3,s:`Each collision happens after the molecule has travelled one mean free path.
Collision frequency = average speed / mean free path = 600/(3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077) = 2 \xD7 10\u2079 per second.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"If the collision frequency of hydrogen molecules in a closed chamber at 27\xB0C is Z, then the collision frequency of the same system at 127\xB0C is",o:["{4;3}Z","{\u221A3;2}Z","{3;4}Z","{2;\u221A3}Z"],c:3,s:`In a closed chamber the number density n is fixed.
Collision frequency = v\u0304/\u03BB \u221D n v\u0304 \u221D \u221AT.
Z\u2082/Z = \u221A(400/300) = 2/\u221A3.
So the new frequency is (2/\u221A3)Z.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"If n is the number density and d is the diameter of the molecule, then the average distance covered by a molecule between two successive collisions (i.e. mean free path) is represented by",o:["{1;\u221A2 n\xB2\u03C0\xB2d\xB2}","{1;\u221A(2n\u03C0d\xB2)}","{1;\u221A2 n\u03C0d\xB2}","\u221A2 n\u03C0d\xB2"],c:2,s:`The mean free path of a gas is \u03BB = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 n d\xB2).
Here n is the number of molecules per unit volume and d is the molecular diameter.
This matches option (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"The number of air molecules per cm\xB3 increased from 3 \xD7 10\xB9\u2079 to 12 \xD7 10\xB9\u2079. The ratio of collision frequency of air molecules before and after the increase in number respectively is",o:["1.25","0.25","0.75","0.50"],c:1,s:`Collision frequency = v\u0304/\u03BB and \u03BB = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n), so frequency \u221D n v\u0304.
Temperature is unchanged, so v\u0304 is the same and frequency \u221D n.
Ratio before/after = 3\xD710\xB9\u2079 / 12\xD710\xB9\u2079 = 0.25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"The mean free path of molecules of a certain gas at STP is 1500d, where d is the diameter of the gas molecules. While maintaining the standard pressure, the mean free path of the molecules at 373 K is approximately",o:["2049d","1098d","1500d","750d"],c:0,s:`\u03BB = kT/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 P). At constant pressure \u03BB \u221D T.
\u03BB\u2082 = \u03BB\u2081 \xD7 T\u2082/T\u2081 = 1500d \xD7 373/273 \u2248 2049d.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"Calculate the value of mean free path (\u03BB) for oxygen molecules at temperature 27\xB0C and pressure 1.01 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa. Assume the molecular diameter 0.3 nm and the gas is ideal. (k = 1.38 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 JK\u207B\xB9)",o:["102 nm","58 nm","86 nm","32 nm"],c:0,s:`\u03BB = kT/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 P) with T = 300 K, d = 0.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m.
Numerator: 1.38\xD710\u207B\xB2\xB3 \xD7 300 = 4.14\xD710\u207B\xB2\xB9.
Denominator: 1.414 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 (0.3\xD710\u207B\u2079)\xB2 \xD7 1.01\xD710\u2075 = 1.414 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 9\xD710\u207B\xB2\u2070 \xD7 1.01\xD710\u2075 \u2248 4.04\xD710\u207B\xB9\u2074.
\u03BB \u2248 1.02 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m \u2248 102 nm.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:`An ideal gas in a closed container is slowly heated. As its temperature increases, which of the following statements are true?
(A) The mean free path of the molecules decreases.
(B) The mean collision time between the molecules decreases.
(C) The mean free path remains unchanged.
(D) The mean collision time remains unchanged.`,o:["(B) and (C)","(C) and (D)","(A) and (B)","(A) and (D)"],c:0,s:`The container is closed, so the number density n is fixed.
Mean free path \u03BB = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n) does not depend on temperature. So (C) is true and (A) is false.
Mean collision time \u03C4 = \u03BB/v\u0304. \u03BB is constant and v\u0304 \u221D \u221AT grows, so \u03C4 decreases. (B) is true and (D) is false.
So (B) and (C) are true.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"In a dilute gas at pressure P and temperature T, the mean time between successive collision of a molecule varies with T as",o:["T","{1;\u221AT}","{1;T}","\u221AT"],c:1,s:`Mean time between collisions \u03C4 = \u03BB/v\u0304 = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n v\u0304).
Taking the gas in a fixed volume (n fixed), \u03C4 \u221D 1/v\u0304.
v\u0304 \u221D \u221AT, so \u03C4 \u221D 1/\u221AT.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"Under an adiabatic process, the volume of an ideal gas gets doubled. Consequently the mean collision time between the gas molecule changes from \u03C4\u2081 to \u03C4\u2082. If {C_P;C_V} = \u03B3 for this gas then a good estimate for {\u03C4\u2081;\u03C4\u2082} is given by",o:["2","{1;2}^\u03B3","{1;2}","{1;2}^({\u03B3+1;2})"],c:3,s:`Mean collision time \u03C4 = \u03BB/v\u0304 with \u03BB \u221D V (n \u221D 1/V) and v\u0304 \u221D \u221AT.
Adiabatic: TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so T \u221D V^(1\u2212\u03B3) and v\u0304 \u221D V^((1\u2212\u03B3)/2).
So \u03C4 \u221D V \xD7 V^((\u03B3\u22121)/2) = V^((\u03B3+1)/2).
When V doubles, \u03C4\u2082/\u03C4\u2081 = 2^((\u03B3+1)/2).
Hence \u03C4\u2081/\u03C4\u2082 = (1/2)^((\u03B3+1)/2), which is option (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"The plot that depicts the behavior of the mean free time \u03C4 (time between two successive collisions) for the molecules of an ideal gas, as a function of temperature (T), qualitatively, is (graphs are schematic and not drawn to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:260,alt:"Four small graphs of mean free time tau: (a) tau against 1/T and (b) tau against 1/root T, each a rising straight line; (c) and (d) tau against T, rising straight lines; line in (b) passes through the origin",d:[["t",14,23,"(a)"],["l",40,109,40,17,"a"],["l",34,105,154,105,"a"],["t",46,29,"\u03C4","s"],["t",154,121,"1/T","e"],["l",50,99,140,45],["t",184,23,"(b)"],["l",210,109,210,17,"a"],["l",204,105,324,105,"a"],["t",216,29,"\u03C4","s"],["t",324,121,"1/\u221AT","e"],["l",210,105,310,43],["t",14,148,"(c)"],["l",40,234,40,142,"a"],["l",34,230,154,230,"a"],["t",46,154,"\u03C4","s"],["t",154,246,"T","e"],["l",50,224,140,170],["t",184,148,"(d)"],["l",210,234,210,142,"a"],["l",204,230,324,230,"a"],["t",216,154,"\u03C4","s"],["t",324,246,"T","e"],["l",220,224,310,170]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`Mean free time \u03C4 = \u03BB/v\u0304. In a closed container \u03BB is fixed, and v\u0304 \u221D \u221AT.
So \u03C4 \u221D 1/\u221AT, which means \u03C4 is proportional to 1/\u221AT.
Plotting \u03C4 against 1/\u221AT gives a straight line through the origin. This is graph (b).
Graphs against 1/T or T would not give a straight line through the origin for this relation.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"A 25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 volume cylinder is filled with 1 mol of O\u2082 gas at room temperature (300 K). The molecular diameter of O\u2082, and its root mean square speed, are found to be 0.3 nm and 200 m/s, respectively. What is the average collision rate (per second) for an O\u2082 molecule?",o:["~10\xB9\xB2","~10\xB9\xB9","~10\xB9\xB3","~10\xB9\u2070"],c:3,s:`Collision rate = v/\u03BB = \u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n v, where n = N/V.
n = 6.02\xD710\xB2\xB3/(25\xD710\u207B\xB3) = 2.4 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075 m\u207B\xB3.
Rate = 1.414 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 (0.3\xD710\u207B\u2079)\xB2 \xD7 2.4\xD710\xB2\u2075 \xD7 200 \u2248 1.9 \xD7 10\u2079 per second.
This is of the order of 10\u2079 to 10\xB9\u2070; the closest given option is ~10\xB9\u2070.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"An ideal gas is enclosed in a cylinder at pressure of 2 atm and temperature, 300 K. The mean time between two successive collisions is 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s. If the pressure is doubled and temperature is increased to 500 K, the mean time between two successive collisions will be close to",o:["4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s","0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 s"],c:0,s:`\u03C4 = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n v\u0304) with n \u221D P/T and v\u0304 \u221D \u221AT.
So \u03C4 \u221D T/(P\u221AT) = \u221AT/P.
\u03C4\u2082 = \u03C4\u2081 \xD7 (P\u2081/P\u2082) \xD7 \u221A(T\u2082/T\u2081) = 6\xD710\u207B\u2078 \xD7 (1/2) \xD7 \u221A(500/300).
\u221A(5/3) = 1.29, so \u03C4\u2082 = 6\xD710\u207B\u2078 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 1.29 \u2248 3.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \u2248 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"Consider an ideal gas confined in an isolated closed chamber. As the gas undergoes an adiabatic expansion, the average time of collision between molecules increases as V^q, where V is the volume of the gas. The value of q is ( \u03B3 = {C_P;C_V} )",o:["{\u03B3+1;2}","{\u03B3\u22121;2}","{3\u03B3+5;6}","{3\u03B3\u22125;6}"],c:0,s:`Average time of collision \u03C4 = \u03BB/v\u0304 with \u03BB \u221D V and v\u0304 \u221D \u221AT.
Adiabatic: T \u221D V^(1\u2212\u03B3), so v\u0304 \u221D V^((1\u2212\u03B3)/2).
\u03C4 \u221D V / V^((1\u2212\u03B3)/2) = V^((\u03B3+1)/2).
So q = (\u03B3+1)/2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Mean free path and collisions",q:"A system consists of two types of gas molecules A and B having same number density 2 \xD7 10\xB2\u2075/m\xB3. The diameter of A and B are 10 \xC5 and 5 \xC5 respectively. They suffer collision at room temperature. The ratio of average distance covered by the molecule A to that of B between two successive collision is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\xB2.",n:25,s:`The average distance between two successive collisions is the mean free path \u03BB = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 d\xB2 n).
Same n, so \u03BB \u221D 1/d\xB2.
\u03BB_A/\u03BB_B = (d_B/d_A)\xB2 = (5/10)\xB2 = 1/4 = 0.25 = 25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2.
So the answer is 25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pqm",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I : The mean free path of gas molecules is inversely proportional to square of molecular diameter.
Statement-II : Average kinetic energy of gas molecules is directly proportional to absolute temperature of gas.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Statement-I is false but Statement-II is true.","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are true.","Statement-I is true but Statement-II is false.","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are false."],c:1,s:`Mean free path \u03BB = 1/(\u221A2 \u03C0 n d\xB2), so \u03BB \u221D 1/d\xB2. Statement-I is true.
Average KE per molecule = (f/2)kT \u221D T. Statement-II is true.
Both statements are true.`}]})();
